<section id="learn" data-section="learn">

<article class="sheet" id="start">
  <div class="prose">
    <div class="eyebrow">Start here</div>
    <h1>Everything in the course, explained from zero</h1>
    <p class="lede">The whole course follows one story. You are thinking about buying <b>Golden State Canning</b>, a small family company that cans fruit, from its owner <b>Mr. Cota</b>. Every chapter answers one question you would ask before buying it.</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><b>How healthy is the company?</b> Ratio analysis (ch. 1).</li>
      <li><b>Where does its cash come from and go?</b> Cash cycle and growth (ch. 2).</li>
      <li><b>How much money will it need in the future?</b> Forecasting (ch. 3).</li>
      <li><b>How do we read the balance sheet like a finance person?</b> Rearranging statements (ch. 4).</li>
      <li><b>How much should it borrow?</b> Capital structure (ch. 5).</li>
      <li><b>Is a new project worth doing?</b> Time value of money and NPV (ch. 6).</li>
      <li><b>Which interest rate should we use to judge it?</b> Risk and return, CAPM, WACC (ch. 7).</li>
      <li><b>So what is the whole company worth?</b> Valuing a business with a DCF (ch. 8).</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Background you need: Mr. Cota ran the company for years, hired an outside manager in 2005–2006 (results got worse), then came back in 2007. All Golden State figures are in <b>thousands of dollars</b>.</p>
    <div class="how">
      <div><b>1 · Read a chapter</b><span>Plain-English idea, formula, worked example, exam traps.</span></div>
      <div><b>2 · Do its exam questions</b><span>150 real questions from the Pre-test and Final Exams 1 and 2, with explanations.</span></div>
      <div><b>3 · Review the formula sheet</b><span>Every formula on one page for the night before.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Golden State's financial statements</h3>
  <p class="prose">You will see these numbers again and again. The <b>income statement</b> shows a full year of activity: sales at the top, then each cost is subtracted until net income. Note that in this course <b>depreciation is subtracted before gross profit</b>.</p>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>Income statement</th><th class="n">2004</th><th class="n">2005</th><th class="n">2006</th><th class="n">2007</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Sales</td><td class="n">808.0</td><td class="n">813.0</td><td class="n">802.0</td><td class="n">842.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>− Cost of goods sold (COGS)</td><td class="n neg">(607.2)</td><td class="n neg">(600.3)</td><td class="n neg">(587.9)</td><td class="n neg">(600.8)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td class="n neg">(23.0)</td><td class="n neg">(21.6)</td><td class="n neg">(37.7)</td><td class="n neg">(39.1)</td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td>Gross profit</td><td class="n">177.8</td><td class="n">191.1</td><td class="n">176.4</td><td class="n">202.1</td></tr>
      <tr><td>− Selling, general &amp; admin (SG&amp;A)</td><td class="n neg">(133.3)</td><td class="n neg">(166.7)</td><td class="n neg">(156.4)</td><td class="n neg">(134.7)</td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td>Operating profit = EBIT</td><td class="n">44.5</td><td class="n">24.4</td><td class="n">20.0</td><td class="n">67.4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>− Interest expense</td><td class="n neg">(6.8)</td><td class="n neg">(8.3)</td><td class="n neg">(9.8)</td><td class="n neg">(8.7)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Profit before tax</td><td class="n">37.7</td><td class="n">16.1</td><td class="n">10.2</td><td class="n">58.7</td></tr>
      <tr><td>− Income taxes</td><td class="n neg">(13.6)</td><td class="n neg">(5.8)</td><td class="n neg">(3.7)</td><td class="n neg">(21.1)</td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td>Net income</td><td class="n">24.1</td><td class="n">10.3</td><td class="n">6.5</td><td class="n">37.6</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table><div class="caption">EBIT = earnings before interest and taxes. Negative numbers are shown in parentheses, as accountants do.</div></div>

  <p class="prose">The <b>balance sheet</b> is a photo taken on one day (December 31). Left side: what the company owns. Right side: who paid for it (lenders and owners). Both sides are always equal.</p>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>Balance sheet, Dec 31</th><th class="n">2003</th><th class="n">2004</th><th class="n">2005</th><th class="n">2006</th><th class="n">2007</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Cash</td><td class="n">4.1</td><td class="n">4.9</td><td class="n">2.9</td><td class="n">1.1</td><td class="n">5.2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Accounts receivable (customers owe us)</td><td class="n">56.9</td><td class="n">61.0</td><td class="n">73.3</td><td class="n">74.7</td><td class="n">70.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Inventories</td><td class="n">8.3</td><td class="n">12.5</td><td class="n">14.1</td><td class="n">16.8</td><td class="n">13.2</td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td>Total current assets</td><td class="n">69.3</td><td class="n">78.4</td><td class="n">90.3</td><td class="n">92.6</td><td class="n">88.9</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Net fixed assets (plant, machines)</td><td class="n">286.6</td><td class="n">304.6</td><td class="n">361.8</td><td class="n">382.0</td><td class="n">374.9</td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td>Total assets</td><td class="n">355.9</td><td class="n">383.0</td><td class="n">452.1</td><td class="n">474.6</td><td class="n">463.8</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Current portion of long-term debt</td><td class="n">3.0</td><td class="n">3.0</td><td class="n">5.0</td><td class="n">5.0</td><td class="n">5.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Accounts payable (we owe suppliers)</td><td class="n">11.8</td><td class="n">14.6</td><td class="n">23.2</td><td class="n">31.6</td><td class="n">17.4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Other accrued expenses</td><td class="n">5.7</td><td class="n">5.5</td><td class="n">4.1</td><td class="n">11.4</td><td class="n">5.2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Revolving credit, bank</td><td class="n">48.4</td><td class="n">51.8</td><td class="n">63.4</td><td class="n">68.6</td><td class="n">45.7</td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td>Total current liabilities</td><td class="n">68.9</td><td class="n">74.9</td><td class="n">95.7</td><td class="n">116.6</td><td class="n">73.3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Long-term (mortgage) debt</td><td class="n">45.0</td><td class="n">42.0</td><td class="n">80.0</td><td class="n">75.0</td><td class="n">70.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Net worth (equity)</td><td class="n">242.0</td><td class="n">266.1</td><td class="n">276.4</td><td class="n">283.0</td><td class="n">320.5</td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td>Total liabilities &amp; net worth</td><td class="n">355.9</td><td class="n">383.0</td><td class="n">452.1</td><td class="n">474.6</td><td class="n">463.8</td></tr>
