<article class="sheet" id="ch1">
<div class="prose">
  <div class="eyebrow">Chapter 1</div>
  <h2>Ratio analysis</h2>
  <p class="lede">A ratio divides one number from the financial statements by another, so you can judge how a company is doing. A ratio on its own means almost nothing: you always <mark>compare it with something</mark>.</p>

  <h3>The six families of ratios</h3>
  <p>The course sorts ratios into six families. Each one answers a simple question:</p>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Main ratios</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Growth</b></td><td>Is the business getting bigger?</td><td>Year-on-year growth, CAGR</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Profitability</b></td><td>How much profit does it make from its sales and capital?</td><td>Gross, operating, net margin; ROA; ROE / ROBE</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Efficiency</b></td><td>How well does it use its assets?</td><td>Asset turnover, inventory turnover, days in inventory, collection period, payables period</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Liquidity</b></td><td>Can it pay what it owes in the next year?</td><td>Current ratio, quick ratio, cash ratio, interval measure</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Leverage</b></td><td>How much debt does it use?</td><td>Debt ratio, debt/equity, equity/assets, times interest earned</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Risk</b></td><td>How uncertain are its results?</td><td>Fixed vs. variable costs</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
<div class="prose">
  <p>One detail matters a lot: when a ratio mixes the income statement (a full year) with the balance sheet (one date), you must say whether you use the balance sheet from the <b>beginning</b>, the <b>end</b> or the <b>average</b> of the year. Exam questions often hide this.</p>

  <h3>Growth</h3>
  <p>Growth is the percentage change of something (sales, profit) from one year to the next. To summarize several years in one number, we use the <b>compound average growth rate (CAGR)</b>: the single constant rate that takes you from the starting value to the ending value.</p>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formulas</div>
    <div class="f">Growth<sub>t</sub> = <span class="frac"><span>X<sub>t</sub> − X<sub>t−1</sub></span><span>X<sub>t−1</sub></span></span></div>
    <div class="f">CAGR = (<span class="frac"><span>End value</span><span>Start value</span></span>)<sup>1/n</sup> − 1</div>
    <p class="words">n is the number of years <b>between</b> the two values. From 2004 to 2007, n = 3.</p>
  </div>
  <h4>Worked example: Golden State sales, 2004 to 2007</h4>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Working</th><th class="n">Result</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Growth 2007</td><td class="w">(842.0 − 802.0) ÷ 802.0</td><td class="n">+5.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Growth 2006</td><td class="w">(802.0 − 813.0) ÷ 813.0</td><td class="n neg">−1.4%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sales CAGR 2004–07</td><td class="w">(842.0 ÷ 808.0)<sup>1/3</sup> − 1</td><td class="n">1.36%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Operating profit CAGR 2004–07</td><td class="w">(67.4 ÷ 44.5)<sup>1/3</sup> − 1</td><td class="n">14.84%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table><div class="caption">Operating profit grew yearly by −45.07%, −18.11%, then +236.73%, yet its CAGR is a smooth 14.84%.</div></div>
<div class="prose">
  <div class="trap"><div class="lab">Exam traps</div><ul>
    <li>n counts the <b>intervals</b>, not the years listed: 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019 is n = 3.</li>
    <li>CAGR only looks at the first and last values. Sales of 50 → 30 → 40 → 50 give a CAGR of <b>0%</b>.</li>
    <li>CAGR is a geometric (compounded) average, not the simple arithmetic average of the yearly rates. It can be positive even if most yearly rates were negative.</li>
  </ul></div>

  <h3>Profitability</h3>
  <p><b>Margins</b> tell you how much of each dollar of sales is left after some costs. Each margin goes one step further down the income statement, so normally <mark>gross margin &gt; operating margin &gt; net margin</mark>.</p>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formulas</div>
    <div class="f">Gross margin = <span class="frac"><span>Gross profit</span><span>Sales</span></span> &nbsp; Operating margin = <span class="frac"><span>EBIT</span><span>Sales</span></span> &nbsp; Net margin = <span class="frac"><span>Net income</span><span>Sales</span></span></div>
    <div class="f">ROE = <span class="frac"><span>Net income</span><span>Equity at end of year</span></span> &nbsp; ROBE = <span class="frac"><span>Net income</span><span>Equity at beginning of year</span></span></div>
    <div class="f">ROA = <span class="frac"><span>Net income</span><span>Total assets</span></span> &nbsp; Pre-tax ROA = <span class="frac"><span>EBIT</span><span>Total assets</span></span></div>
    <p class="words">ROBE (return on beginning equity) compares a year's profit with the money the owners had invested at the start of that year.</p>
  </div>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>Golden State 2007</th><th>Working</th><th class="n">Result</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Gross margin</td><td class="w">202.1 ÷ 842.0</td><td class="n">24.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Operating margin</td><td class="w">67.4 ÷ 842.0</td><td class="n">8.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Net margin</td><td class="w">37.6 ÷ 842.0</td><td class="n">4.5%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ROE (ending equity)</td><td class="w">37.6 ÷ 320.5</td><td class="n">11.7%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ROBE (beginning equity = 2006)</td><td class="w">37.6 ÷ 283.0</td><td class="n">13.3%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pre-tax ROA</td><td class="w">67.4 ÷ 463.8</td><td class="n">14.5%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
<div class="prose">
  <div class="trap"><div class="lab">Exam traps</div><ul>
    <li>Exams love "what happens if…" questions. Walk down the income statement. Example: if <b>depreciation falls</b> (sales and other costs unchanged), gross profit rises (depreciation sits above it), operating profit rises, net income rises, and ROBE rises because beginning equity does not change.</li>
    <li>ROBE uses <b>last year's</b> equity. Firm Z: net income 40, equity 250 (2017) and 300 (2018). ROBE 2018 = 40 ÷ 250 = 16.0%, not 40 ÷ 300.</li>
    <li>EBIT and operating profit are the same thing.</li>
  </ul></div>

  <h3>Efficiency</h3>
  <p>Efficiency ratios measure how hard the company makes its assets work. A <b>turnover</b> says how many times something "turns" in a year; a <b>days</b> ratio says how many days of activity it represents. They are two views of the same thing.</p>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formulas</div>
    <div class="f">Asset turnover = <span class="frac"><span>Sales</span><span>Total assets</span></span></div>
    <div class="f">Inventory turnover = <span class="frac"><span>COGS</span><span>Inventory</span></span> &nbsp; Days in inventory = <span class="frac"><span>Inventory</span><span>COGS ÷ 365</span></span></div>
    <div class="f">Receivables turnover = <span class="frac"><span>Sales</span><span>Receivables</span></span> &nbsp; Collection period = <span class="frac"><span>Receivables</span><span>Sales ÷ 365</span></span></div>
    <div class="f">Payables period = <span class="frac"><span>Payables</span><span>COGS ÷ 365</span></span></div>
    <p class="words">Inventory and payables are compared with <b>COGS</b> (they are valued at cost). Receivables are compared with <b>sales</b> (customers owe the selling price). The course assumes credit purchases = COGS.</p>
  </div>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>Golden State 2007</th><th>Working</th><th class="n">Result</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Asset turnover, beginning assets</td><td class="w">842.0 ÷ 474.6</td><td class="n">1.77×</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Asset turnover, ending assets</td><td class="w">842.0 ÷ 463.8</td><td class="n">1.82×</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Asset turnover, average assets</td><td class="w">842.0 ÷ [(463.8 + 474.6) ÷ 2]</td><td class="n">1.79×</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Inventory turnover</td><td class="w">600.8 ÷ 13.2</td><td class="n">45.5×</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Days in inventory</td><td class="w">13.2 ÷ (600.8 ÷ 365)</td><td class="n">8.0 days</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Collection period</td><td class="w">70.5 ÷ (842.0 ÷ 365)</td><td class="n">30.6 days</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Payables period</td><td class="w">17.4 ÷ (600.8 ÷ 365)</td><td class="n">10.6 days</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table><div class="caption">8 days of inventory looks tiny because the balance sheet is dated December 31, long after the harvest peak. That is seasonality.</div></div>
<div class="prose">
  <div class="trap"><div class="lab">Exam traps</div><ul>
    <li>You cannot compute days in inventory with sales and operating profit only: you need COGS ("not enough information").</li>
    <li>Which ratio has sales in the denominator? The <b>collection period</b> (receivables ÷ daily sales). Receivables turnover has sales on top.</li>
    <li>From the collection period you can recover sales: sales = receivables × 365 ÷ days. 242 × 365 ÷ 93 = $950.</li>
    <li>High asset turnover with low margins is typical of <b>supermarkets</b>.</li>
  </ul></div>

  <h3>Liquidity</h3>
  <p>Liquidity is the ability to pay bills due within a year. We compare liquid assets with current liabilities.</p>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formulas</div>
    <div class="f">Current ratio = <span class="frac"><span>Current assets</span><span>Current liabilities</span></span></div>
    <div class="f">Quick ratio = <span class="frac"><span>Cash + marketable securities + receivables</span><span>Current liabilities</span></span></div>
    <div class="f">Cash ratio = <span class="frac"><span>Cash (+ marketable securities)</span><span>Current liabilities</span></span></div>
    <div class="f">Interval measure = <span class="frac"><span>Cash + marketable securities + receivables</span><span>Average daily operating costs</span></span></div>
    <p class="words">The quick ratio leaves out inventory because it is the least liquid current asset. The interval measure says how many days the firm could keep running on its liquid assets alone.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Golden State: current ratio 2007 = 88.9 ÷ 73.3 = <b>1.21</b> (it was only 0.79 in 2006, under the hired manager). Quick ratio 2007 = (5.2 + 70.5) ÷ 73.3 = <b>1.03</b>. A common rule of thumb is a current ratio above 1.</p>

  <h3>Leverage</h3>
  <p>Leverage (or "gearing") measures how much the company is financed with debt. Debt creates fixed payments (interest and principal) that make profits more volatile.</p>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formulas</div>
    <div class="f">Debt ratio = <span class="frac"><span>Interest-bearing debt</span><span>Interest-bearing debt + Equity</span></span> &nbsp; Debt/equity = <span class="frac"><span>Debt</span><span>Equity</span></span></div>
    <div class="f">Equity/assets = <span class="frac"><span>Equity</span><span>Total assets</span></span> = 1 − <span class="frac"><span>Liabilities</span><span>Total assets</span></span></div>
    <div class="f">Times interest earned = <span class="frac"><span>EBIT</span><span>Interest expense</span></span></div>
  </div>
  <p>Golden State 2007: interest-bearing debt = 5.0 + 45.7 + 70.0 = 120.7. Debt ratio = 120.7 ÷ (120.7 + 320.5) = <b>27.4%</b>. Equity/assets = 320.5 ÷ 463.8 = <b>0.69</b>. Times interest earned = 67.4 ÷ 8.7 = <b>7.7×</b>.</p>
  <p>Leverage and liquidity are different things and do not move together: a firm can have lots of long-term debt and plenty of cash.</p>

  <h3>Risk</h3>
  <p>The course's risk indicator is the ratio of <b>fixed costs to variable costs</b>. Fixed costs stay the same whatever the sales; variable costs move with sales. More fixed costs means more operating risk, but also <b>operating leverage</b>: when sales grow, profits grow faster.</p>

  <h3>Common-size statements</h3>
  <p>A common-size statement turns every line into a percentage so that size disappears. Balance sheet lines are divided by <b>total assets</b>; income statement lines by <b>sales</b>. This lets you compare companies of different sizes and spot trends.</p>
  <p>Example (Billie's Baubles, from the exams): receivables 70 ÷ total assets 190 = <b>36.8%</b>. Golden State 2007: COGS = 71.4% of sales, net fixed assets = 80.8% of total assets.</p>
  <p>If you know two common-size numbers you can compare them directly: receivables 25% and payables 40% of the same total assets means current ratio = 25 ÷ 40 &lt; 1.</p>

  <h3>How to interpret, and the limits</h3>
  <p>Compare a ratio with: the company's <b>own past</b> (time trend), <b>peers</b> in the same industry, its <b>budget</b> or targets, and <b>rules of thumb</b>. Limits to quote in an exam:</p>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>Financial statements miss things like market share.</li>
    <li>Truly similar companies are hard to find.</li>
    <li>Accounting methods differ across companies and countries, and can change over time.</li>
    <li>One-off events, discontinued operations and non-operating items distort ratios.</li>
    <li>Seasonality and business cycles distort ratios.</li>
    <li>Most ratios use book values, not market values.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="goprac"><button class="btn" data-goch="1" type="button">Practice chapter 1 questions</button><span class="muted" data-count="1"></span></div>
</div>
</article>

<!-- ======================= CH 2 ======================= -->
<article class="sheet" id="ch2">
<div class="prose">
  <div class="eyebrow">Chapter 2</div>
  <h2>Cash cycle and growth</h2>
  <p class="lede">A profitable company can still go bankrupt if it runs out of cash. Profit follows accounting rules; cash is what actually pays the bills. <mark>Growth makes the cash problem worse.</mark></p>

  <h3>The cash cycle</h3>
  <p>The cash cycle (also called the operating cycle or the net working capital cycle) is the loop: buy fruit and cans → hold them as inventory → sell to customers on credit → collect the cash → buy again. Golden State must pay for peaches and cans <b>before</b> customers pay it. That gap must be financed by someone (the bank, suppliers, or the owners).</p>
  <p>When a firm grows, the gap grows too: it uses cash from yesterday's (smaller) sales to buy for tomorrow's (bigger) sales. Some of the cycle comes from the nature of the business (canning is seasonal), but management choices matter too (how much credit to give customers, how much inventory to hold).</p>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formula</div>
    <div class="f">Cash conversion cycle (CCC) = Days in inventory + Collection period − Payables period</div>
    <p class="words">Days the company's own cash is tied up: money waits in inventory, then in receivables, minus the days suppliers let you wait before paying them.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Golden State 2007: 8.0 + 30.6 − 10.6 = <b>28 days</b>. A bank lends dollars, not days, so you then translate the days into an amount: the bigger the business, the bigger the loan for the same number of days.</p>
  <p><b>To shorten the cycle:</b> hold less inventory, collect from customers faster, or pay suppliers later. If you know three of the four numbers you can solve for the last one: 27 = 110 + 64 − payables period, so payables period = <b>147 days</b>.</p>
  <div class="trap"><div class="lab">Exam traps</div><ul>
    <li>More days in inventory or a longer collection period make the cycle <b>longer</b> (worse). A longer payables period makes it <b>shorter</b> (better).</li>
    <li>Collecting from customers faster than you pay suppliers <b>improves</b> the cycle. Billie's Baubles collects in 64 days and pays in 146 days: that is good, not bad.</li>
  </ul></div>

  <h3>Sources and uses of funds</h3>
  <p>A quick way to see where money came from and where it went: take two balance sheets, compute the change in every line, and label each change.</p>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>On an asset</th><th>On a liability or equity</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Increase</b></td><td>Use (cash was spent on it)</td><td>Source (someone gave you money)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Decrease</b></td><td>Source (it turned into cash)</td><td>Use (you paid someone back)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table><div class="caption">Because balance sheets balance, total sources always equal total uses.</div></div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>Golden State, 2006 → 2007 (Mr. Cota is back)</th><th class="n">Sources</th><th class="n">Uses</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Receivables fell 74.7 → 70.5</td><td class="n">4.2</td><td class="n"></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Inventory fell 16.8 → 13.2</td><td class="n">3.6</td><td class="n"></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Net fixed assets fell 382.0 → 374.9</td><td class="n">7.1</td><td class="n"></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Equity rose 283.0 → 320.5 (retained profit)</td><td class="n">37.5</td><td class="n"></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cash rose 1.1 → 5.2</td><td class="n"></td><td class="n">4.1</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Payables fell 31.6 → 17.4</td><td class="n"></td><td class="n">14.2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Accrued expenses fell 11.4 → 5.2</td><td class="n"></td><td class="n">6.2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Revolving bank credit fell 68.6 → 45.7</td><td class="n"></td><td class="n">22.9</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mortgage fell 75.0 → 70.0</td><td class="n"></td><td class="n">5.0</td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td>Total</td><td class="n">52.4</td><td class="n">52.4</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table><div class="caption">Reading: in 2007 Mr. Cota collected receivables, cut inventory and kept his profit, and used that cash to pay suppliers and the bank. Under the hired manager (2004 → 2006) it was the opposite: fixed assets rose by 77.4, financed mostly by more debt and more supplier credit.</div></div>
<div class="prose">

  <h3>How fast can the company grow?</h3>
  <p>Two benchmark growth rates tell you how fast a firm can grow with its own profits. First, the <b>retention rate</b> b (also called the <b>plowback ratio</b>) is the share of profit kept in the firm instead of paid as dividends: b = 1 − payout ratio.</p>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formulas</div>
    <div class="f">Internal growth rate = <span class="frac"><span>Net income − Dividends</span><span>Assets at beginning</span></span> = b × ROBA</div>
    <div class="f">Sustainable growth rate (SGR) = b × ROBE</div>
    <p class="words">Internal growth: no outside money at all. Sustainable growth: <b>no new shares</b>, and operating <b>and financing</b> ratios stay constant, so the firm borrows just enough to keep its debt ratio unchanged. That extra borrowing is why SGR is higher than the internal rate for a firm with debt.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Worked example (Final Exam 2): retention rate 50%, sales 25,000, profit margin 10%, beginning equity 50,000. Net income = 10% × 25,000 = 2,500. ROBE = 2,500 ÷ 50,000 = 5%. <b>SGR = 50% × 5% = 2.5%.</b> The asset turnover and debt figures in the question are distractors.</p>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">DuPont formula</div>
    <div class="f">ROBE = <span class="frac"><span>Net income</span><span>Sales</span></span> × <span class="frac"><span>Sales</span><span>Beginning assets</span></span> × <span class="frac"><span>Beginning assets</span><span>Beginning equity</span></span></div>
    <p class="words">Profitability × efficiency × leverage. To raise ROBE (and so SGR), improve one of the three.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="trap"><div class="lab">Exam traps</div><ul>
    <li>The SGR is a <b>benchmark, not a maximum</b>. A firm can grow faster by improving margins, turnover or leverage, or by issuing new shares.</li>
    <li>Reducing an asset is a <b>source</b>; increasing an asset is a <b>use</b>.</li>
    <li>Profit is not cash: "profitable but can't pay its bills" is possible (True).</li>
  </ul></div>
  <div class="goprac"><button class="btn" data-goch="2" type="button">Practice chapter 2 questions</button><span class="muted" data-count="2"></span></div>
</div>
</article>

<!-- ======================= CH 3 ======================= -->
<article class="sheet" id="ch3">
<div class="prose">
  <div class="eyebrow">Chapter 3</div>
  <h2>Financial forecasting</h2>
  <p class="lede">Forecasting means building <b>pro forma</b> statements: future income statements and balance sheets. Their most common job is to answer one question: <mark>how much outside money will the plan need?</mark></p>

  <h3>What goes into a forecast</h3>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li><b>A business forecast</b>, e.g. "sales grow 6% next year", consistent with the strategy.</li>
    <li><b>Management policies</b>: how much inventory to keep, credit terms, marketing and R&amp;D spending.</li>
    <li><b>Economic factors</b>: inflation, interest rates, exchange rates.</li>
    <li><b>Accounting rules</b>: the balance sheet must balance; retained earnings = last year's + net income − dividends; fixed assets = last year's + capital spending − depreciation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><b>Errors vs. biases.</b> Errors are random mistakes that tend to cancel out over many forecasts. Biases are systematic over- or under-estimates that do not cancel out. Unintended biases should be removed; deliberate ones (a "worst case") should be explained.</p>

  <h3>The external funding need</h3>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formula</div>
    <div class="f">External funding need = Projected total assets − (Projected liabilities + Projected equity)</div>
    <p class="words">Positive: the plan needs new money (debt or shares). Negative: the plan generates a surplus.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>The percent-of-sales method</h3>
  <p>The simplest method assumes many accounts move in proportion to sales. Three steps:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>Sort the accounts. <b>Vary with sales:</b> COGS, SG&amp;A, receivables, inventory, payables. <b>Don't vary with sales:</b> depreciation, interest, bank debt, long-term debt, equity (set separately).</li>
    <li>For each account that varies, compute its usual % of sales from past years.</li>
    <li>Apply that % to the forecast sales.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Payables can also be projected from a target payables period (payables = COGS ÷ 365 × target days). Both ways are acceptable.</p>

  <h3>The interest problem (circularity)</h3>
  <p>Interest depends on how much you borrow. How much you borrow depends on the funding need. The funding need depends on net income, which depends on interest. The way out is to iterate:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>Guess the interest expense.</li>
    <li>Compute the funding need that results.</li>
    <li>Assume that need is borrowed and recompute interest (the course uses 8%).</li>
    <li>Repeat until the interest you get equals the interest you guessed.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><b>Seasonality:</b> a pro forma dated December 31 can show a surplus while the firm needs a lot of money at harvest time. Seasonal firms forecast monthly or quarterly.</p>

  <h3>Golden State's expansion plan</h3>
  <p>If you buy the company, you plan to grow it: cold storage to extend the canning season, new gourmet products, regional distribution, and the jarring line. Key assumptions: existing lines grow 15% a year, COGS 72% of sales (75% for new lines), SG&amp;A 19–21% of sales, $40k a year of maintenance capital spending.</p>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>$ thousands</th><th class="n">2008</th><th class="n">2009</th><th class="n">2010</th><th class="n">2011</th><th class="n">2012</th><th class="n">2013</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Total sales</td><td class="n">892.5</td><td class="n">1,176.4</td><td class="n">1,480.3</td><td class="n">1,882.4</td><td class="n">2,161.0</td><td class="n">2,470.1</td></tr>
      <tr><td>of which new lines</td><td class="n">0</td><td class="n">150.0</td><td class="n">300.0</td><td class="n">525.0</td><td class="n">600.0</td><td class="n">675.0</td></tr>
