<div class="prose">
  <h3>Testing a forecast</h3>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What changes</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Sensitivity analysis</b></td><td>One assumption at a time, everything else fixed</td><td>Sales grow 3% instead of 6%</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Scenario analysis</b></td><td>Several assumptions together, telling one coherent story</td><td>A competitor closes half its stores: your sales rise and your COGS rises with them</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Simulation</b></td><td>Probability distributions, run by computer</td><td>Monte Carlo simulation</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
<div class="prose">
  <div class="trap"><div class="lab">Exam traps</div><ul>
    <li>"Changing one assumption at a time" is <b>sensitivity</b> analysis, not scenario analysis.</li>
    <li>Depreciation does <b>not</b> vary directly with sales (it depends on fixed assets).</li>
    <li>A pro forma balance sheet projects assets, liabilities and equity; revenues and costs belong to the pro forma income statement. Pro formas follow normal accounting rules.</li>
    <li>Biases cannot always be eliminated: some are deliberate.</li>
  </ul></div>
