<!-- ======================= CH 4 ======================= -->
<article class="sheet" id="ch4">
<div class="prose">
  <div class="eyebrow">Chapter 4</div>
  <h2>Rearranging financial statements</h2>
  <p class="lede">Finance people regroup the accounting statements into two blocks: what <b>creates</b> cash (real activities) and what <b>hands cash to investors</b> (financial activities). Accounting is not wrong; this view is just more useful for valuation.</p>

  <h3>Real vs. financial</h3>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li><b>Real activities</b> produce and sell goods: production, sales, R&amp;D, marketing. Their balance sheet items: inventory, receivables, fixed assets, and also <b>accounts payable and accrued operating expenses</b> (they come from running the business).</li>
    <li><b>Financial activities</b> deal with lenders and shareholders: interest, principal, dividends, issuing debt or shares. Their balance sheet items: <b>interest-bearing debt</b> (short and long term) and equity.</li>
    <li>On the income statement: sales, COGS, R&amp;D, marketing are real; interest is financial; <b>income tax is both</b>, because it depends on operating profit (real) and is reduced by interest (financial).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The simplified balance sheet</h3>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Identity</div>
    <div class="f">Net working capital (NWC) + Net fixed assets (NFA) = Debt + Equity</div>
    <p class="words">NWC = cash + receivables + inventory − payables − accrued expenses. Payables are subtracted on the left because they are operating, not financing.</p>
  </div>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>Real side (Dec 31, 2007)</th><th class="n">$k</th><th>Financial side</th><th class="n">$k</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Cash</td><td class="n">5.2</td><td>Current portion of LTD</td><td class="n">5.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td class="n">70.5</td><td>Revolving credit</td><td class="n">45.7</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Inventories</td><td class="n">13.2</td><td>Mortgage</td><td class="n">70.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Accounts payable</td><td class="n neg">(17.4)</td><td><b>Total debt</b></td><td class="n"><b>120.7</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Other accrued expenses</td><td class="n neg">(5.2)</td><td><b>Equity</b></td><td class="n"><b>320.5</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Net working capital</b></td><td class="n"><b>66.3</b></td><td></td><td class="n"></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Net fixed assets</b></td><td class="n"><b>374.9</b></td><td></td><td class="n"></td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td>Total real</td><td class="n">441.2</td><td>Total financial</td><td class="n">441.2</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table><div class="caption">The total (441.2) is smaller than accounting total assets (463.8), so ratios like asset turnover change: always say which definition you use.</div></div>
<div class="prose">
  <p><b>Why bother?</b> At a glance you see how capital-intensive the company is (fixed assets vs. working capital) and how it is financed. This is the view used for free cash flow and for the debt/equity weights in chapters 7 and 8.</p>
  <p><b>Tricky items:</b> excess cash and unused land go in a separate box; convertible bonds are part debt, part equity; employee stock options are both real (pay) and financial (securities); "miscellaneous" accounts can be hard to place.</p>
