</div>
</article>

<!-- ======================= CH 5 ======================= -->
<article class="sheet" id="ch5">
<div class="prose">
  <div class="eyebrow">Chapter 5</div>
  <h2>Capital structure</h2>
  <p class="lede">Capital structure is the mix of debt and equity a company uses to pay for its assets. The goal is to <mark>maximize the value of the firm</mark>, which is the same as minimizing its cost of capital.</p>

  <h3>Debt vs. equity</h3>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Debt</th><th>Equity</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>What it is</b></td><td>A loan: the company <b>borrows</b> and must <b>pay</b> interest and principal</td><td>Ownership of the company</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Type of claim</b></td><td>Fixed claim, set by contract</td><td>Residual claim: whatever is left after lenders</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>If not paid</b></td><td>Default: lenders get rights. Default does not automatically mean bankruptcy</td><td>Dividends can be skipped legally; the board decides them</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Bankruptcy</b></td><td>Paid first</td><td>Paid last, can end up worth zero</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Control</b></td><td>Covenants (e.g. debt/equity must stay below 0.5)</td><td>Votes for the board and big decisions, not day-to-day</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Risk and cost</b></td><td>Less risky, so cheaper</td><td>More risky, so more expensive</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table><div class="caption">Debt features: seniority (who is paid first), security/collateral, covenants, callability (repay early). Limited liability: shareholders can lose their stake, not their house.</div></div>
<div class="prose">

  <h3>Leverage and ROE</h3>
  <p>Debt adds a fixed cost (interest). When business is good, owners keep everything above the interest, so ROE is higher. When business is bad, the interest must still be paid, so ROE falls further. <mark>Leverage amplifies ROE in both directions.</mark> The business risk does not disappear: it is concentrated on fewer equity dollars (the "conservation of business risk").</p>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formula</div>
    <div class="f">Net income = (EBIT − Interest) × (1 − t)</div>
  </div>
  <p>Illustration (my numbers, no tax): two firms with $1,000 of assets. Firm U is all equity. Firm L has $500 debt at 8% (interest 40) and $500 equity.</p>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>EBIT</th><th class="n">ROE, unlevered</th><th class="n">ROE, levered</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Good year: 150</td><td class="n">150 ÷ 1,000 = 15%</td><td class="n">(150 − 40) ÷ 500 = 22%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bad year: 50</td><td class="n">50 ÷ 1,000 = 5%</td><td class="n">(50 − 40) ÷ 500 = 2%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table><div class="caption">Higher expected ROE with debt, but a wider swing: that is the extra risk owners are paid for.</div></div>
<div class="prose">

  <h3>Modigliani &amp; Miller, Proposition 1 (1958)</h3>
  <p>In a perfect market, <b>the value of a firm does not depend on its capital structure</b>, only on its assets. Equity gets riskier with debt, but its required return rises by exactly enough to compensate. Five conditions:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>No taxes.</li>
    <li>No costs of financial distress.</li>
    <li>Symmetric information (everyone knows the same things).</li>
    <li>Complete markets (everyone can borrow and lend on the same terms).</li>
    <li>Investment decisions do not change with financing.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><b>Why it must hold: arbitrage.</b> If an indebted firm were worth more than an identical debt-free firm, you would sell shares of the expensive (levered) one and buy shares of the cheap (unlevered) one, borrowing on your own to copy the leverage. That is a risk-free profit, so prices adjust until values are equal.</p>
  <p>Every other theory comes from <b>relaxing</b> one of these conditions. Under M&amp;M, maximizing firm value and minimizing the cost of capital are the same thing.</p>

  <h3>Relaxing "no taxes": the tax shield</h3>
  <p>Interest is tax-deductible, dividends are not. So each dollar of interest saves t dollars of tax: this is the <b>interest tax shield</b>.</p>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formulas</div>
    <div class="f">Interest tax shield = Interest × t</div>
    <div class="f">V<sub>L</sub> = V<sub>U</sub> + PV(tax shields) &nbsp; (permanent debt: PV(tax shields) = t × D)</div>
    <p class="words">Billie's Baubles: interest 7, tax 35%, tax shield = 7 × 0.35 = $2.45. With taxes only, the "best" structure would be 100% debt, which is unrealistic.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Relaxing "no distress costs"</h3>
  <p><b>Costs of financial distress (CFD)</b> are the extra costs a firm suffers when it cannot pay, or almost cannot pay, its debt.</p>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li><b>Direct:</b> attorney and advisor fees, court costs.</li>
    <li><b>Indirect:</b> customers and suppliers who lose trust, employees who leave, distracted managers.</li>
    <li>Only count costs caused by the debt, not the recession itself.</li>
    <li>Lower for firms with tangible, resellable assets (a hotel chain) than for firms whose value is people (an ad agency).</li>
    <li>The single biggest driver of the probability of distress is <b>the amount of leverage</b>.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Static Tradeoff Model</div>
    <div class="f">PV(CFD) = Probability of distress × PV(costs of distress)</div>
    <div class="f">V<sub>L</sub> = V<sub>U</sub> + PV(tax shields) − PV(CFD)</div>
    <p class="words">It relaxes two conditions: <b>taxes</b> and <b>costs of financial distress</b>. Debt helps (tax shield) until distress costs outweigh it, so there is an optimum. Problem: nobody can measure CFD well, so the optimum cannot be located in practice; tax shields are probably overestimated (personal taxes, other tax shields like depreciation).</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Other theories</h3>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Theory</th><th>M&amp;M condition relaxed</th><th>Idea</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Pecking order</b></td><td>Symmetric information</td><td>Insiders know more, so outsiders charge more for capital, especially equity. Order: <b>internal funds → debt → equity</b>. No single optimal debt ratio. Profitable firms fund themselves and end up with less debt.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Agency costs</b></td><td>Interests aligned</td><td>Agents (managers) may shirk or take value from principals (owners). Near bankruptcy, shareholders gamble with what is really the lenders' money (firm owes 50,000, assets worth 40,000). Debt also reduces managers' free cash and discretion.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Product market</b></td><td>Investment unchanged by financing</td><td>Keep debt low so you can credibly fight a new competitor.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Control &amp; governance</b></td><td></td><td>A family firm borrows rather than issue shares, to keep its votes.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table><div class="caption">No model is universally accepted. In the real world, the optimal structure depends on the firm and on economic conditions.</div></div>
<div class="prose">

  <h3>Payout policy</h3>
  <p>Two ways to give cash to shareholders: <b>dividends</b> and <b>share buybacks</b>. Without taxes, shareholders are indifferent. When dividends are taxed more than capital gains (course: 30% vs. 15%), buybacks are preferred.</p>
  <p>When a dividend is paid, the share price falls by the dividend. Example: you bought at $47, the price is $53, a $2 dividend is paid. New price $51, dividend income $2, unrealized gain 51 − 47 = $4.</p>
  <p>In practice mature firms pay more dividends, young growing firms pay little. Dividends are smoothed over time because investors read them as a signal; buybacks vary with the cycle.</p>
  <div class="trap"><div class="lab">Exam traps</div><ul>
    <li>Interest payments are legally required; dividends are not.</li>
    <li>Debt <b>adds</b> fixed costs; it does not lower them.</li>
    <li>"Incomplete markets" is not an M&amp;M condition (complete markets is).</li>
    <li>The pecking order does <b>not</b> imply a unique optimal structure; the static tradeoff does.</li>
    <li>As EBIT drops, a levered firm's ROE drops <b>more</b> than an unlevered firm's.</li>
  </ul></div>
  <div class="goprac"><button class="btn" data-goch="5" type="button">Practice chapter 5 questions</button><span class="muted" data-count="5"></span></div>
</div>
</article>

<!-- ======================= CH 6 ======================= -->
<article class="sheet" id="ch6">
<div class="prose">
  <div class="eyebrow">Chapter 6</div>
  <h2>Time value of money and project valuation</h2>
  <p class="lede">A dollar today is worth more than a dollar tomorrow, because today's dollar can be invested and earn interest. To compare money received at different dates, you move everything to the same date.</p>

  <h3>Compounding and discounting</h3>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formulas</div>
    <div class="f">Future value: FV = PV × (1 + r)<sup>t</sup></div>
    <div class="f">Present value: PV = <span class="frac"><span>FV</span><span>(1 + r)<sup>t</sup></span></span></div>
    <div class="f">Stream of cash flows: PV = <span class="frac"><span>CF<sub>1</sub></span><span>(1 + r)</span></span> + <span class="frac"><span>CF<sub>2</sub></span><span>(1 + r)<sup>2</sup></span></span> + … + <span class="frac"><span>CF<sub>t</sub></span><span>(1 + r)<sup>t</sup></span></span></div>
    <p class="words">Compounding earns interest on interest. Discounting is the reverse: dividing by (1 + r) for each year of waiting.</p>
  </div>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>Exercise</th><th>Working</th><th class="n">Answer</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>$400 for 7 years at 7%</td><td class="w">400 × 1.07<sup>7</sup></td><td class="n">$642.31</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Need $5,000 in 15 years at 12%</td><td class="w">5,000 ÷ 1.12<sup>15</sup></td><td class="n">$913.48</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Need $25,000 in 5 years at 8%</td><td class="w">25,000 ÷ 1.08<sup>5</sup></td><td class="n">$17,014.58</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Interest earned on $2,000, 3 years at 5%</td><td class="w">2,000 × (1.05<sup>3</sup> − 1)</td><td class="n">$315.25</td></tr>
      <tr><td>$100 at 5%, 6%, 7%, 8%, 9%</td><td class="w">100 × 1.05 × 1.06 × 1.07 × 1.08 × 1.09</td><td class="n">$140.19</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table><div class="caption">Simple interest (no interest on interest) gives the wrong answers $596 and $300: classic distractors.</div></div>
<div class="prose">

  <h3>Perpetuities</h3>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formulas</div>
    <div class="f">Perpetuity: PV = <span class="frac"><span>CF</span><span>r</span></span> &nbsp;&nbsp; Growing perpetuity: PV = <span class="frac"><span>CF<sub>1</sub></span><span>r − g</span></span></div>
    <p class="words">The first payment arrives in one year. g must be smaller than r. Examples: $100 at 5% → $2,000. $40 growing 4%, discounted at 7% → 40 ÷ 0.03 = $1,333.33. Working backwards: $50 a year worth $454.55 → r = 50 ÷ 454.55 = 11%.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>The jarring line project</h3>
  <p>Golden State owns a jarring line. Keep and run it, or sell it now for <b>$10,000</b>? To decide, we count only <b>incremental cash flows</b>: cash that exists only because of the project. Raw materials and sales commissions are incremental costs; the project's sales are revenue.</p>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>Each year</th><th>Working</th><th class="n">$</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Extra sales</td><td class="w">5,000 jars × $3.50</td><td class="n">17,500</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Gross profit</td><td class="w">23% of sales</td><td class="n">4,025</td></tr>
      <tr><td>− Sales commissions</td><td class="w">3% × 17,500</td><td class="n neg">(525)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>= Profit before depreciation</td><td class="w"></td><td class="n">3,500</td></tr>
      <tr><td>− Depreciation</td><td class="w">non-cash charge</td><td class="n neg">(2,000)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>= Taxable income</td><td class="w"></td><td class="n">1,500</td></tr>
      <tr><td>− Tax</td><td class="w">36% × 1,500</td><td class="n neg">(540)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>= Net income</td><td class="w"></td><td class="n">960</td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td>Operating cash flow</td><td class="w">960 + 2,000 depreciation added back</td><td class="n">2,960</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
<div class="prose">
  <p>Three ways to get the same 2,960 (know all three):</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>Gross profit − commissions − tax = 4,025 − 525 − 540.</li>
    <li>3,500 × (1 − 0.36) + <b>depreciation tax shield</b> 2,000 × 0.36 = 2,240 + 720.</li>
    <li>Net income + depreciation = 960 + 2,000.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Depreciation tax shield</div>
    <div class="f">Depreciation tax shield = Depreciation × t</div>
    <p class="words">A $100,000 machine depreciated straight-line over 8 years: 12,500 a year × 36% = $4,500 a year.</p>
  </div>
  <p>The line also needs $800 of extra inventory in year 1 (2009), so the first cash flow is 2,960 − 800 = 2,160. Cash flows start one year from now.</p>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th></th><th class="n">2009</th><th class="n">2010</th><th class="n">2011</th><th class="n">2012</th><th class="n">2013</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Cash flow</td><td class="n">2,160</td><td class="n">2,960</td><td class="n">2,960</td><td class="n">2,960</td><td class="n">2,960</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Discount factor 1 ÷ 1.08<sup>t</sup></td><td class="n">0.926</td><td class="n">0.857</td><td class="n">0.794</td><td class="n">0.735</td><td class="n">0.681</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Present value</td><td class="n">2,000</td><td class="n">2,538</td><td class="n">2,350</td><td class="n">2,176</td><td class="n">2,015</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cumulative PV</td><td class="n">2,000</td><td class="n">4,538</td><td class="n">6,887</td><td class="n">9,063</td><td class="n">11,078</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cumulative cash (not discounted)</td><td class="n">2,160</td><td class="n">5,120</td><td class="n">8,080</td><td class="n">11,040</td><td class="n">14,000</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
<div class="prose">

  <h3>The four decision criteria</h3>
  <h4>Net present value (NPV)</h4>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formula</div>
    <div class="f">NPV = <span class="frac"><span>CF<sub>1</sub></span><span>(1 + r)</span></span> + … + <span class="frac"><span>CF<sub>t</sub></span><span>(1 + r)<sup>t</sup></span></span> − Initial investment</div>
    <p class="words">Jarring line: NPV = 11,078 − 10,000 = <b>$1,078 &gt; 0, keep the line</b>. The right comparison is the $10,000 sale price you give up, not zero.</p>
  </div>
  <p><b>Rule:</b> accept if NPV &gt; 0, reject if NPV &lt; 0; NPV = 0 is the marginal case. NPV is the amount by which the project increases the firm's value. The rule assumes the goal is value maximization. To compute it you need: the investment outlays, the discount rate, the incremental cash flows and their timing.</p>
  <h4>Payback period</h4>
  <p>The time to get the investment back, without discounting. Jarring line: 8,080 recovered by end-2011, 1,920 still missing, 2,960 ÷ 12 = 247 a month, so about 8 more months: <b>3 years 8 months</b>. Simple, needs no discount rate, but ignores time value and every cash flow after the payback date. A project can pay back fast and still have negative NPV.</p>
  <h4>Discounted payback</h4>
  <p>Same idea with discounted cash flows: <b>about 4.5 years</b> for the jarring line. Includes time value but still ignores later cash flows.</p>
  <h4>Internal rate of return (IRR)</h4>
  <p>The discount rate that makes NPV = 0. Accept if IRR &gt; cost of capital. Jarring line: at 10% NPV is still +493, so the IRR is higher; Excel gives <b>11.8%</b> &gt; 8%. NPV falls as the rate rises, so if NPV is negative at 8%, the IRR is below 8%.</p>
  <div class="trap"><div class="lab">Exam traps</div><ul>
    <li>IRR problems: <b>several IRRs</b> when cash flow signs change more than once; IRR assumes cash is reinvested at the IRR. There is only ever one NPV for a given rate.</li>
    <li>Positive NPV ⇔ IRR above the discount rate (for a normal project).</li>
    <li>"The beginning of year 2" = "the end of year 1": discount once.</li>
    <li>Payback of a project that pays only in year 2 is exactly 2 years, not less.</li>
  </ul></div>
  <div class="goprac"><button class="btn" data-goch="6" type="button">Practice chapter 6 questions</button><span class="muted" data-count="6"></span></div>
</div>
</article>

<!-- ======================= CH 7 ======================= -->
<article class="sheet" id="ch7">
<div class="prose">
  <div class="eyebrow">Chapter 7</div>
  <h2>Risk and return</h2>
  <p class="lede">Real cash flows are uncertain. We handle that in two places: the cash flow on top becomes an <b>expected</b> cash flow, and the discount rate below gets a <b>risk premium</b>. The riskier the project, the higher the rate.</p>

  <h3>Expected cash flow and the risk premium</h3>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formulas</div>
    <div class="f">E(CF) = Σ (probability × cash flow)</div>
    <div class="f">k = r<sub>f</sub> + risk premium</div>
    <p class="words">E(CF) weights each outcome by its probability (not a simple average). The mode is the single most likely outcome. r<sub>f</sub> is the risk-free rate (a government bond).</p>
  </div>
  <p>If project B is riskier than A with the same expected cash flows, investors will only buy B at a <b>lower price</b>, which gives B a <b>higher expected return</b> and a <b>higher risk premium</b>.</p>
  <p><b>Opportunity cost of capital</b> = the expected return you could earn on another investment <b>with the same risk</b>. So there is no "free" capital: money lent to you at 0% still costs what you could have earned elsewhere. This is why the discount rate comes from the capital markets.</p>

  <h3>Measuring risk: variance and diversification</h3>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formulas</div>
    <div class="f">Variance σ<sup>2</sup> = E[(X − E(X))<sup>2</sup>] &nbsp;&nbsp; Volatility σ = √variance</div>
    <div class="f">Var(aA + bB) = a<sup>2</sup>Var(A) + b<sup>2</sup>Var(B) + 2ab Cov(A,B)</div>
    <div class="f">Correlation ρ = <span class="frac"><span>Cov(A,B)</span><span>σ<sub>A</sub> σ<sub>B</sub></span></span> &nbsp; always between −1 and +1</div>
  </div>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>A portfolio's <b>expected return</b> is the weighted average of the assets' returns.</li>
    <li>Its <b>standard deviation is not</b>: it is lower whenever ρ &lt; 1. That is the benefit of diversification. With ρ = +1 there is no benefit; with ρ = −1 risk can be removed completely.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The CAPM picture</h3>
  <p>CAPM assumptions: investors want to maximize wealth, are <b>risk averse</b>, look only at <b>risk and expected return</b>, and returns are normally distributed (bell curve). It does not assume investors minimize risk whatever the return, nor that they are long-term.</p>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>Plot all possible portfolios (expected return vs. standard deviation). The best ones lie on the <b>efficient frontier</b>. The course calls the portfolio with the highest return for a given risk the <b>optimal portfolio</b>.</li>
    <li>Add the risk-free asset (σ = 0). The line from it that just touches the frontier is the <b>Capital Market Line (CML)</b>; it touches at the <b>market portfolio</b>.</li>
    <li>On the CML, investors <b>left</b> of the market portfolio hold some T-bills: they are <b>lenders</b>. Investors to the <b>right</b> borrow at r<sub>f</sub> to invest more than 100%: they are <b>borrowers</b>.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="fig" id="learnfig"></div>
  <p>For one stock, only the risk you cannot diversify away (<b>systematic risk</b>) earns a premium. It is measured by <b>beta</b>. The <b>Security Market Line</b> plots expected return against beta.</p>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">CAPM</div>
    <div class="f">E(r<sub>x</sub>) = r<sub>f</sub> + β<sub>x</sub> × (R<sub>m</sub> − r<sub>f</sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp; β<sub>x</sub> = <span class="frac"><span>Cov(r<sub>x</sub>, R<sub>m</sub>)</span><span>Var(R<sub>m</sub>)</span></span></div>
    <p class="words">The market's β is 1; a T-bill's β is 0. β 1.5 means the stock tends to move 1.5× the market (market −20% → stock −30%). The equity market risk premium (R<sub>m</sub> − r<sub>f</sub>) is usually 4–6%; the course uses <b>5%</b>.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Example: r<sub>f</sub> 5%, β 1.2, expected market return 12%. E(r) = 5% + 1.2 × (12% − 5%) = <b>13.4%</b>. Careful: 12% is the market return, so the premium is 7%.</p>

  <h3>From Smucker to Golden State: finding the discount rate</h3>
  <p>Golden State is not listed on the stock market, so it has no beta. We borrow one from a similar listed company, J.M. Smucker (jams and fruit spreads). But Smucker's beta includes Smucker's own debt, so we first remove the debt effect (<b>unlever</b>), then add Golden State's debt (<b>relever</b>).</p>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Course formulas (simplified, no taxes)</div>
    <div class="f">β<sub>unlevered</sub> = <span class="frac"><span>E</span><span>V</span></span> × β<sub>equity</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; β<sub>relevered</sub> = <span class="frac"><span>β<sub>unlevered</sub></span><span>E ÷ V</span></span></div>
    <div class="f">K<sub>operations</sub> = <span class="frac"><span>D</span><span>V</span></span>K<sub>d</sub> + <span class="frac"><span>E</span><span>V</span></span>K<sub>e</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; R<sub>assets</sub> = r<sub>f</sub> + β<sub>unlevered</sub> × EMRP</div>
    <div class="f">WACC = <span class="frac"><span>D</span><span>V</span></span> × K<sub>d</sub> × (1 − t) + <span class="frac"><span>E</span><span>V</span></span> × K<sub>e</sub></div>
    <p class="words">V = D + E at <b>market values</b> (share price × number of shares). The (1 − t) applies only to debt, because interest is tax-deductible.</p>
  </div>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Smucker</th><th>Golden State</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Debt D</td><td class="w">$695m (book ≈ market)</td><td class="w">target 60% of V</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Equity E</td><td class="w">$56.10 × 56.78m shares = $3,185m</td><td class="w">target 40% of V</td></tr>
      <tr><td>D/V ; E/V</td><td class="w">18% ; 82%</td><td class="w">60% ; 40%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Equity beta</td><td class="w">0.82 (observed)</td><td class="w">0.67 ÷ 0.40 = <b>1.68</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Unlevered beta</td><td class="w">0.82 × 0.82 = <b>0.67</b></td><td class="w">0.67 (same business)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost of equity Ke = 5.6% + β × 5%</td><td class="w"><b>9.7%</b></td><td class="w"><b>14.0%</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost of debt Kd</td><td class="w">7.0%</td><td class="w">8.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pre-tax cost of capital K<sub>operations</sub></td><td class="w">9.2%</td><td class="w">0.6 × 8% + 0.4 × 14% = <b>10.4%</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td>WACC, t = 36%</td><td class="w">8.76%</td><td class="w">0.6 × 8% × 0.64 + 0.4 × 14% = <b>8.67%</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td>R<sub>assets</sub> (unlevered)</td><td class="w" colspan="2">5.6% + 0.67 × 5% = <b>8.95% ≈ 9.0%</b></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
<div class="prose">
  <p>What to remember: Golden State's cost of equity is much higher than Smucker's (14.0% vs. 9.7%) <b>only because of its debt</b>. After tax, the two WACCs are almost identical (8.67% vs. 8.76%) because the businesses have the same risk. With debt, the WACC ends up <b>below</b> the unlevered cost of equity, thanks to the tax shield.</p>
  <p>Three candidate discount rates: R<sub>assets</sub>, K<sub>operations</sub> (pre-tax WACC) and the WACC. The WACC is the most used, for cash flows as risky as the company as a whole, but it is not always the right choice.</p>
  <div class="trap"><div class="lab">Exam traps</div><ul>
    <li>More debt → equity beta <b>rises</b> → cost of equity <b>rises</b>.</li>
    <li>Unlevering: 0.90 × 5,000 ÷ 6,000 = <b>0.75</b> (multiply by E/V, don't divide).</li>
    <li>WACC with 30% debt, Kd 7%, Ke 15%, t 36%: 0.3 × 7% × 0.64 + 0.7 × 15% = <b>11.8%</b>.</li>
    <li>The current ratio has nothing to do with the WACC.</li>
    <li>Beta measures <b>systematic</b> (non-diversifiable) risk, not total risk.</li>
  </ul></div>
  <div class="goprac"><button class="btn" data-goch="7" type="button">Practice chapter 7 questions</button><span class="muted" data-count="7"></span></div>
</div>
</article>

<!-- ======================= CH 8 ======================= -->
<article class="sheet" id="ch8">
<div class="prose">
  <div class="eyebrow">Chapter 8</div>
  <h2>Valuing a business</h2>
  <p class="lede">Everything comes together: forecast Golden State's cash flows, discount them at the WACC, add a value for the years after the forecast. Result: <mark>about $1.02 million</mark>.</p>

  <h3>Three approaches</h3>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>How it works</th><th>Weakness</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Balance sheet</b></td><td>Value every asset at market value (intangibles too), subtract operating liabilities, then debt</td><td>Misses the extra value created by combining the assets well (the positive NPV)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Market</b> (multiples)</td><td>Apply a multiple of sales, earnings or cash flow seen in similar companies or deals</td><td>Only as good as the sample of comparables</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Income</b> (DCF)</td><td>Present value of future cash flows</td><td>Very sensitive to assumptions, especially the terminal value</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
<div class="prose">
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">DCF value</div>
    <div class="f">Value = <span class="frac"><span>FCF<sub>1</sub></span><span>(1 + k)</span></span> + … + <span class="frac"><span>FCF<sub>T</sub></span><span>(1 + k)<sup>T</sup></span></span> + <span class="frac"><span>TV<sub>T</sub></span><span>(1 + k)<sup>T</sup></span></span></div>
    <p class="words">Three ingredients: (1) forecasts of free cash flow, (2) a discount rate reflecting time value and systematic risk, (3) a terminal value TV for everything after year T.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>1. Enterprise free cash flow</h3>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formula</div>
    <div class="f">FCF = EBIT × (1 − t) + Depreciation − Capital expenditures − Δ Net working capital</div>
    <p class="words">Start from operating profit taxed as if there were no debt. Add back depreciation (not cash). Subtract the cash spent on new fixed assets and on extra working capital.</p>
  </div>
  <p><b>Not included:</b> interest, dividends, debt repayments, share issues. FCF is the cash the business generates <b>before</b> it is shared out to lenders and shareholders (left side of the rearranged balance sheet).</p>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>Exam example (Billie's Baubles)</th><th>Working</th><th class="n">$</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>EBIT × (1 − t)</td><td class="w">150 × (1 − 0.35)</td><td class="n">97.50</td></tr>
      <tr><td>+ Depreciation</td><td class="w"></td><td class="n">50.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td>− Capital expenditures</td><td class="w"></td><td class="n neg">(30.00)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>− Δ NWC</td><td class="w"></td><td class="n neg">(25.00)</td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td>Free cash flow</td><td class="w"></td><td class="n">92.50</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table><div class="caption">Working backwards also comes up: FCF 31 = 40 × 0.65 + D − 20 − 5, so depreciation D = 30.</div></div>
<div class="prose">

  <h3>2. Discount rate</h3>
  <p>The course uses the <b>WACC = 8.7%</b>. Not the cost of equity (14%) or the cost of debt (8%) alone: neither reflects the whole company. Alternatives for the sensitivity test: R<sub>assets</sub> 9.0%, pre-tax cost of capital 10.4%.</p>

  <h3>3. Terminal value</h3>
  <p>The forecast stops in 2013, but the company keeps running. The terminal value is the company's value at the end of 2013, usually computed as a growing perpetuity.</p>
  <div class="formula"><div class="lab">Formula</div>
    <div class="f">TV<sub>2013</sub> = <span class="frac"><span>FCF<sub>2014</sub></span><span>k − g</span></span> &nbsp;&nbsp; with FCF<sub>2014</sub> = FCF<sub>2013</sub> × (1 + g)</div>
    <p class="words">g must be below k. Reasonable benchmarks for g: long-term GDP growth, long-term inflation, the long-term risk-free rate.</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="clean">
    <li>Naive version: 54.5 × 1.03 ÷ (0.087 − 0.03) ≈ <b>990</b>. But 2013 cash flow is held down by heavy investment.</li>
    <li>So we use a <b>normalized</b> 2014 cash flow of 88.0 (adjusted margins, capex, depreciation, NWC): 88.0 ÷ (0.087 − 0.03) = <b>1,543</b> (the course rounds to 1,540).</li>
    <li>Cautious version with g = 0: normalized FCF = EBIT(1 − t), since depreciation = capex and no extra NWC. 88 ÷ 0.087 ≈ <b>1,000</b>.</li>
    <li>Same cash flow, other growth rates: g 2% → 1,313; g 3% → 1,544; g 4% → 1,872.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The terminal value is most of the total value, so it deserves the most scrutiny.</p>

  <h3>Putting it together</h3>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>$ thousands</th><th class="n">2008</th><th class="n">2009</th><th class="n">2010</th><th class="n">2011</th><th class="n">2012</th><th class="n">2013</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Free cash flow</td><td class="n">45.0</td><td class="n neg">(36.2)</td><td class="n neg">(6.1)</td><td class="n">18.1</td><td class="n">47.3</td><td class="n">54.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>+ Terminal value</td><td class="n"></td><td class="n"></td><td class="n"></td><td class="n"></td><td class="n"></td><td class="n">1,540.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Discount factor 1 ÷ 1.087<sup>t</sup></td><td class="n">0.920</td><td class="n">0.846</td><td class="n">0.779</td><td class="n">0.716</td><td class="n">0.659</td><td class="n">0.606</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Present value</td><td class="n">41.4</td><td class="n neg">(30.6)</td><td class="n neg">(4.8)</td><td class="n">13.0</td><td class="n">31.2</td><td class="n">966.6</td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td>DCF value</td><td colspan="6" class="n">1,016.8 ≈ $1.02 million</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table><div class="caption">The course counts 2008 as year 1. Cash flows are negative in 2009–2010 because of the expansion spending.</div></div>
<div class="prose">

  <h3>Sensitivity analysis</h3>
</div>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table class="ledger">
    <thead><tr><th>What if…</th><th class="n">Value ($k)</th><th class="n">Change</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Base case: existing lines grow 15%, WACC 8.7%</td><td class="n">1,020</td><td class="n"></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Existing lines grow 10% instead of 15% (terminal value 1,091.9)</td><td class="n">716.8</td><td class="n neg">−29.5%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>WACC 8.0% instead of 8.7% (terminal value 1,912.6)</td><td class="n">1,291</td><td class="n">+27%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
<div class="prose">
  <p>Other "what if" questions: growth too optimistic? Premium prices impossible? New products cost more or come late? The hard part is knowing which inputs deserve the most attention.</p>
  <p><b>What the number means:</b> $1.02m is what the business is worth <b>to you</b>, with your plan. It is not automatically the price to offer. The NPV of the purchase = value of the company − price paid. Buying for less than it is worth to you creates value for you.</p>
