{s:"F1",n:1,c:1,q:"Choose the option to correctly complete the following sentence. Operating margin is calculated as:",o:["earnings before interest expense and income tax expense divided by sales.","earnings before interest expense and income tax expense divided by net worth.","net income divided by sales.","net income divided by net worth."],a:0,x:"Operating margin = operating profit (EBIT) ÷ sales. Net income ÷ sales is the net margin; net income ÷ net worth is ROE."},
{s:"F1",n:2,c:1,fig:"BIS",q:"Presented below is the 2019 income statement for Billie’s Baubles. Which of the following statements are true?<span class='stmt'>I. Net margin is less than operating margin.<br>II. Operating margin is less than gross margin.<br>III. Gross margin is less than net margin.</span>",o:["I only","II only","I and II, but not III","I, II, and III"],a:2,x:"Gross margin = 250 ÷ 400 = 62.5%. Operating margin = 150 ÷ 400 = 37.5%. Net margin = 103 ÷ 400 = 25.75%. So net &lt; operating &lt; gross: I and II are true, III is false."},
{s:"F1",n:3,c:1,fig:"BBS",q:"Presented below is the 2019 balance sheet for Billie’s Baubles. What was Billie’s Baubles’ current ratio in 2019?",o:["0.55","1.14","1.43","2.31"],a:2,x:"Current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities = 150 ÷ 105 = 1.43. (1.14 is the quick ratio.)"},
{s:"F1",n:4,c:1,q:"A firm has the following data for 2019: sales $500, depreciation $30, interest $50, tax $30, net worth $100 (net worth in 2018 was also $100). In 2020, sales, COGS and SG&amp;A stay the same; depreciation, interest expense and tax expense all decrease. Which statements are true for 2020?<span class='stmt'>I. Gross margin will decrease.<br>II. Operating margin will increase.<br>III. Net income will decrease.<br>IV. Return on beginning equity will increase.</span>",o:["I only","I and II only","II and IV only","III and IV only"],a:2,x:"Walk down the income statement. Lower depreciation: gross profit rises (depreciation sits above gross profit), so I is false; operating profit rises, so II is true. Lower depreciation, interest and tax all raise net income, so III is false. Beginning equity is still 100, so higher net income means higher ROBE: IV is true."},
{s:"F1",n:5,c:1,fig:"BBS",q:"Presented below is the 2019 balance sheet for Billie’s Baubles. For Billie’s Baubles, common size accounts receivable is equal to ____ and common size revolving credit is equal to ____.",o:["21.0%; 36.8%","21.0%; 34.2%","34.2%; 21.0%","36.8%; 21.0%"],a:3,x:"A common-size balance sheet divides each line by total assets. Receivables = 70 ÷ 190 = 36.8%. Revolving bank debt = 40 ÷ 190 = 21.0%."},
{s:"F1",n:6,c:1,q:"Company A’s only current asset is accounts receivable and its only current liability is accounts payable. Common size accounts receivable is 25% and common size accounts payable is 40%. Company A’s current ratio ____.",o:["is less than 1.","is equal to 1.","is greater than 1.","cannot be determined from the information given."],a:0,x:"Both numbers are percentages of the same total assets, so current ratio = 25% ÷ 40% = 0.625, which is below 1."},
{s:"F1",n:7,c:1,q:"A firm has the following data for 2019: sales $300, depreciation $20, interest $40, tax $40, net worth $150 (also $150 in 2018). In 2020, sales, COGS and SG&amp;A stay the same, depreciation increases, interest expense increases, and tax expense decreases. Which is true?<span class='stmt'>I. Gross margin will decrease.<br>II. Operating margin will decrease.<br>III. Net income will increase.<br>IV. Return on beginning equity will increase.</span>",o:["I only","I and II only","I, II and III only","I, II, III, and IV"],a:1,x:"Higher depreciation lowers gross profit and operating profit: I and II are true. Depreciation and interest both rise, which cuts profit before tax; the lower tax only partly offsets it, so net income falls (III false) and ROBE falls (IV false)."},
{s:"F1",n:8,c:1,fig:"BBS",q:"Presented below is the 2019 balance sheet for Billie’s Baubles. The cash ratio is equal to ____ and the quick ratio is equal to ____.",o:["0.476; 1.143","1.143; 0.476","1.429; 1.143","1.429; 0.476"],a:0,x:"Cash ratio = cash ÷ current liabilities = 50 ÷ 105 = 0.476. Quick ratio = (cash + receivables) ÷ current liabilities = 120 ÷ 105 = 1.143. Inventory is excluded from the quick ratio."},
{s:"F1",n:9,c:1,fig:"XYZ",q:"Company XYZ’s sales data are presented below. What is Company XYZ’s three-year compound average growth rate in 2019?",o:["0%","7.7%","18.6%","29.1%"],a:0,x:"CAGR = (50 ÷ 50)<sup>1/3</sup> − 1 = 0%. CAGR only uses the first and the last values; the dip in between does not matter."},
{s:"F1",n:10,c:1,q:"Firm Z had net income of $40 and net worth of $300 in 2018. Its net worth in 2017 was $250. What was Firm Z’s return on beginning equity in 2018?",o:["6.25%","13.3%","16.0%","83.3%"],a:2,x:"ROBE = net income ÷ equity at the beginning of the year (end of 2017) = 40 ÷ 250 = 16.0%. 40 ÷ 300 = 13.3% would be ROE (ending equity): a trap."},
{s:"F1",n:11,c:2,q:"It is possible for a firm to be profitable from an accounting perspective even if it has difficulty meeting its financial obligations.",o:TF,a:0,x:"Profit follows accounting rules, not cash. A profitable firm, especially a fast-growing one, can run out of cash because it pays suppliers before customers pay it."},
{s:"F1",n:12,c:2,q:"Which of the following is an example of a source of funds?",o:["Reduction in inventory","Increase in accounts receivable","Increase in goodwill","Purchase of fixed assets"],a:0,x:"Reducing an asset turns it into cash: source. Increasing receivables, goodwill or fixed assets absorbs cash: uses."},
{s:"F1",n:13,c:2,q:"A firm’s retention rate:<span class='stmt'>I. is the fraction of earnings that is retained by the company.<br>II. is sometimes referred to as the plowback ratio.<br>III. is equal to 1 minus the payout ratio.</span>",o:["I only","II only","I and II only","I, II, and III"],a:3,x:"All three are true: it is the share of profit kept in the firm, also called the plowback ratio, and equals 1 − payout ratio."},
{s:"F1",n:14,c:2,q:"A firm can improve its cash conversion cycle by:<span class='stmt'>I. increasing its collection period.<br>II. increasing its payables period.<br>III. increasing days in inventory.</span>",o:["I only","II only","I and III only","I, II, and III"],a:1,x:"CCC = days in inventory + collection period − payables period. Only a longer payables period (paying suppliers later) shortens it. I and III lengthen it."},
{s:"F1",n:15,c:2,q:"In general, a decrease in an asset is considered a use of funds.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. A decrease in an asset releases cash, so it is a source. Uses are increases in assets or decreases in liabilities and equity."},
{s:"F1",n:16,c:2,q:"A firm’s sustainable growth rate:<span class='stmt'>I. is the rate at which a company can grow if it does not issue any new external equity.<br>II. is the rate at which a company can grow if it keeps its operating and financing ratios constant.<br>III. is equal to the retention rate times return on beginning equity.</span>",o:["I only","I and II only","I and III only","I, II, and III"],a:3,x:"All three are the course definition: no new shares, constant operating and financing ratios, and SGR = b × ROBE."},
{s:"F1",n:17,c:3,q:"In financial forecasting, ____ are simply mistakes that tend to cancel out over time, but ____ are systematic tendencies to under or overestimate, and do not generally cancel out over time.",o:["biases; errors","errors; biases","errors; conditional forecasts","conditional forecasts; biases"],a:1,x:"Errors are random and cancel out over many forecasts. Biases are systematic and do not."},
{s:"F1",n:18,c:3,q:"A pro forma balance sheet can help a manager estimate the external financing the firm needs. The formula is:",o:["projected total assets – projected liabilities + equity","projected total assets – (projected liabilities + equity)","projected liabilities + equity – projected total assets","projected liabilities – equity – projected total assets"],a:1,x:"External funding need = projected total assets − (projected liabilities + equity). Watch the parentheses: the first option adds equity instead of subtracting it."},
{s:"F1",n:19,c:3,q:"When estimating a pro forma balance sheet, it is appropriate to forecast accounts payable:<span class='stmt'>I. as a percent of sales.<br>II. using a targeted efficiency ratio, such as the expected payables period.</span>",o:["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],a:2,x:"Both work. Percent of sales is the simple method; a target payables period gives payables = COGS ÷ 365 × target days."},
{s:"F1",n:20,c:4,q:"When rearranging financial statements, which of the following items is included in financial activities?",o:["Marketing expense","Research and development expense","Interest expense","Sales revenue"],a:2,x:"Financial activities are dealings with investors: interest, principal, dividends, issuing debt or shares. Marketing, R&amp;D and sales are real activities."},
{s:"F1",n:21,c:4,q:"Which of the following items on the income statement is associated with both real and financial activities?",o:["Sales revenue","Research and development expense","Interest expense","Income tax expense"],a:3,x:"Income tax depends on operating profit (real) and is reduced by the interest deduction (financial), so it belongs to both."},
{s:"F1",n:22,c:5,q:"Which of the following is a key characteristic of an equity security?",o:["Firms are contractually obligated to pay dividends on equity.","Equity represents a loan to the firm.","Equity represents ownership in a firm.","Equity has priority over debt in bankruptcy."],a:2,x:"Equity is ownership and a residual claim. Dividends are not required, equity is not a loan, and debt is paid first in bankruptcy."},
{s:"F1",n:23,c:5,q:"A company that issues debt as part of its capital structure is agreeing to ____ money and ____ interest on the debt.",o:["borrow; pay","borrow; receive","lend; receive","lend; pay"],a:0,x:"Issuing debt means the company borrows money and pays interest on it."},
{s:"F1",n:24,c:5,q:"Which of the following items increases return on beginning equity (ROBE), holding all else equal?",o:["Beginning of year net worth","Net income","Interest expense","Taxes"],a:1,x:"ROBE = net income ÷ beginning equity. More net income raises it; more beginning equity, interest or taxes lower it."},
{s:"F1",n:25,c:5,q:"Assume M&amp;M Proposition I holds. If the value of a levered firm (VL) is higher than the value of an otherwise identical unlevered firm (VU), an arbitrage opportunity exists for an investor who owns stock in both. This arbitrage involves:",o:["selling some shares in the levered firm and simultaneously buying some shares in the unlevered firm.","selling some shares in the unlevered firm and simultaneously buying some shares in the levered firm.","selling some shares in both firms.","buying more shares in both firms."],a:0,x:"The levered firm is overpriced: sell it, buy the cheaper unlevered firm (borrowing personally to copy the leverage). You lock in a risk-free profit, which pushes the two values back together."},
{s:"F1",n:26,c:5,q:"In most countries:",o:["corporate interest expenses are not tax-deductible while corporate dividend payments are tax-deductible.","corporate interest expenses are tax-deductible while corporate dividend payments are not tax-deductible.","both corporate interest expenses and corporate dividend payments are tax deductible.","neither corporate interest expenses nor corporate dividend payments are tax deductible."],a:1,x:"Interest is deductible, dividends are not. That difference creates the interest tax shield."},
{s:"F1",n:27,c:5,q:"For a potential investor, investing in a firm’s debt is less risky than investing in its equity because:<span class='stmt'>I. principal and interest payments are contractually obligated.<br>II. debtholders are paid back before equity holders in case of bankruptcy.<br>III. a firm that includes debt in its capital structure has lower fixed costs than an all-equity firm.</span>",o:["I only","I and II only","I and III only","I, II, and III"],a:1,x:"I and II explain why debt is safer. III is false: debt adds fixed costs (interest), it does not reduce them."},
{s:"F1",n:28,c:5,q:"In the real world (relaxing the assumptions of M&amp;M Proposition I), optimal capital structure is:",o:["0% debt/100% equity.","50% debt/50% equity.","100% debt/0% equity.","dependent on firm and general economic conditions."],a:3,x:"With taxes, distress costs and other frictions, the best mix depends on each firm (its assets, profits, risk) and on the economy. No single number fits everyone."},
{s:"F1",n:29,c:5,q:"If a firm defaults on its debt, it is required to file bankruptcy.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. A default does not automatically lead to bankruptcy: the firm can renegotiate or restructure with its lenders."},
{s:"F1",n:30,c:5,q:"According to the course, which is the single largest contributor to the probability that a firm ends up in financial distress?",o:["Broad economic conditions","The firm’s industry","The amount of leverage in the firm’s capital structure","The competence of the firm’s management"],a:2,x:"The course says the biggest driver of the probability of distress is how much the firm borrows."},
{s:"F1",n:31,c:5,q:"Which two M&amp;M Proposition I conditions are relaxed in the Static Tradeoff Model?",o:["Costs of financial distress and symmetric information","Costs of financial distress and taxes","Costs of financial distress and complete markets","Costs of financial distress and invariant investment behavior"],a:1,x:"The Static Tradeoff Model adds taxes (the benefit of debt) and costs of financial distress (the cost of debt) and balances them."},
{s:"F1",n:32,c:5,q:"According to the Pecking Order theory, which order does a firm prefer when accessing its funding sources?",o:["Debt, equity, internal funds","Equity, debt, internal funds","Internal funds, debt, equity","Internal funds, equity, debt"],a:2,x:"Internal funds first, then debt, equity last. Outsiders fear that insiders sell shares when they are overpriced, so equity is the most expensive."},
{s:"F1",n:33,c:5,q:"A firm owes its creditors $50,000 but its assets are only worth $40,000. Equity holders want very risky projects (if they do nothing they are wiped out), while debt holders want to avoid them. Which theory represents this?",o:["The static tradeoff theory","An agency cost","A product market model","The asymmetric information of debtholders and equity holders"],a:1,x:"An agency cost between shareholders and creditors: near insolvency, shareholders gamble with what is really the lenders' money."},
{s:"F1",n:34,c:5,q:"To maintain family control over voting shares, a family-controlled business may avoid issuing equity even if it would otherwise make sense to do so.",o:TF,a:0,x:"True: control and governance. New shares would dilute the family's votes, so it borrows more instead."},
{s:"F1",n:35,c:6,q:"A perpetuity pays $50 per year forever. If its present value is $454.55, what is the discount rate?",o:["8%","9%","10%","11%"],a:3,x:"PV = CF ÷ r, so r = 50 ÷ 454.55 = 11%."},
{s:"F1",n:36,c:6,q:"A project requires $40 today. Expected cash flow is $0 in year 1 and $50 in year 2. The discount rate is 9%. What is the NPV?",o:["$2.08","$42.08","$50.00","$100"],a:0,x:"NPV = −40 + 0 ÷ 1.09 + 50 ÷ 1.09<sup>2</sup> = −40 + 42.08 = $2.08."},
{s:"F1",n:37,c:6,q:"Which NPV decision criteria are true?<span class='stmt'>I. Accept projects for which NPV &gt; 0.<br>II. Projects for which NPV &lt; 0 are marginal.<br>III. Reject projects for which NPV = 0.</span>",o:["I only","II only","II and III only","I, II, and III"],a:0,x:"Accept if NPV &gt; 0, reject if NPV &lt; 0, and NPV = 0 is the marginal case. Statements II and III swap the last two."},
{s:"F1",n:38,c:6,q:"You invest $400 today for 7 years at 7%, compounded annually. How much will you have at the end?",o:["$596.00","$600.29","$642.31","$680.00"],a:2,x:"FV = 400 × 1.07<sup>7</sup> = $642.31. $596 is simple interest (no interest on interest)."},
{s:"F1",n:39,c:6,q:"You want $5,000 in 15 years. How much must you invest today at 12% compounded annually?",o:["$333.33","$913.48","$4,464.29","$26,751.25"],a:1,x:"PV = 5,000 ÷ 1.12<sup>15</sup> = 5,000 ÷ 5.474 = $913.48."},
{s:"F1",n:40,c:8,q:"A firm has EBIT of $40, a tax rate of 35%, free cash flow of $31, capital expenditures of $20 and a change in net working capital of $5. What is its depreciation expense?",o:["$0","$20","$25","$30"],a:3,x:"FCF = EBIT(1 − t) + depreciation − capex − ΔNWC. 31 = 40 × 0.65 + D − 20 − 5 = 26 + D − 25, so D = 30."},
{s:"F1",n:41,c:6,q:"A firm is considering a project to produce a new product. Which items are potential incremental costs?<span class='stmt'>I. Sales of the new product<br>II. Raw materials needed as input<br>III. Commissions to the sales team to sell the new product</span>",o:["I only","I and II only","II and III only","I, II, and III"],a:2,x:"Incremental costs are extra costs caused by the project: raw materials and sales commissions (like the jarring line's 3% commission). Sales are revenue, not a cost."},
{s:"F1",n:42,c:6,q:"It is possible for the Payback Period criterion to accept a project while the NPV criterion rejects it.",o:TF,a:0,x:"True. Payback ignores time value and all cash flows after the cutoff, so a project can pay back fast and still have negative NPV."},
{s:"F1",n:43,c:6,q:"An investment pays $40 in year 1, growing at 4% a year forever. The discount rate is 7%. What is its present value?",o:["$400.00","$571.43","$1000.00","$1333.33"],a:3,x:"Growing perpetuity: PV = CF<sub>1</sub> ÷ (r − g) = 40 ÷ (0.07 − 0.04) = $1,333.33."},
{s:"F1",n:44,c:7,q:"Project B is riskier than Project A. Therefore B should have a ____ price, a ____ expected return, and a ____ risk premium than A.",o:["higher; higher; higher","higher; higher; lower","lower; higher; higher","lower; lower; higher"],a:2,x:"Investors want more return for more risk. With the same expected cash flows, the riskier project must sell at a lower price, which gives a higher expected return and a higher risk premium."},
{s:"F1",n:45,c:7,q:"Which of the following is NOT an assumption of the CAPM?",o:["Investors are risk averse: they prefer the less risky of two otherwise identical investments.","Any risky investment can be described by the risk it entails and the expected return it offers.","Returns on risky assets have normal (bell-shaped) distributions.","An investor’s goal is to minimize his risk, even if this results in lower expected returns."],a:3,x:"CAPM investors trade risk off against return and maximize wealth; they do not minimize risk whatever it costs. The other three are CAPM assumptions."},
{s:"F1",n:46,c:7,q:"An equally weighted portfolio of two risky assets A and B gives diversification benefits (lower volatility) as long as their correlation coefficient is:",o:["1","greater than 1","less than 1","not equal to zero"],a:2,x:"Any correlation below +1 reduces portfolio volatility. Only perfectly correlated assets give no benefit. (A correlation can never exceed 1.)"},
{s:"F1",n:47,c:7,fig:"CML",q:"In the following plot, the line drawn tangent to the efficient frontier is called the:",o:["Capital allocation line.","Capital market line.","Security characteristic line.","Security market line."],a:1,x:"The line from the risk-free asset tangent to the efficient frontier at the market portfolio is the Capital Market Line. The Security Market Line plots return against beta, not standard deviation."},
{s:"F1",n:48,c:7,q:"A company has a beta of 0.90. The market value of its equity is $5,000 and the market value of the company is $6,000. What is its unlevered beta?",o:["0.75","0.90","1.00","1.08"],a:0,x:"Course formula: β unlevered = (E ÷ V) × β equity = (5,000 ÷ 6,000) × 0.90 = 0.75."},
{s:"F1",n:49,c:7,q:"Debt is 30% of the capital structure, cost of debt 7%, cost of equity 15%, tax rate 36%. What is the WACC?",o:["11.0%","11.3%","11.8%","12.6%"],a:2,x:"WACC = 0.30 × 7% × (1 − 0.36) + 0.70 × 15% = 1.34% + 10.50% = 11.8%."},
{s:"F1",n:50,c:7,q:"The correlation coefficient between any two stocks is constrained to be:",o:["between –1 and 1.","between –1 and 0.","between 0 and 1.","less than 1."],a:0,x:"ρ = Cov(A,B) ÷ (σ<sub>A</sub>σ<sub>B</sub>) always lies between −1 and +1."},

/* ===== Final Exam 2 ===== */
{s:"F2",n:1,c:5,q:"An increase in financial leverage generally results in a higher return on equity (ROE).",o:TF,a:0,x:"True. More debt raises expected ROE (as long as assets earn more than the debt costs), and also makes ROE more volatile."},
{s:"F2",n:2,c:1,q:"Leverage and liquidity generally rise or fall together.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. Leverage measures debt in the capital structure; liquidity measures the ability to pay short-term bills. They are unrelated: a firm can have heavy long-term debt and lots of cash."},
{s:"F2",n:3,c:2,q:"It is possible for a company to grow faster than its sustainable growth rate.",o:TF,a:0,x:"True. The SGR is a benchmark, not a ceiling: improve margins, turnover or leverage, or issue new shares."},
{s:"F2",n:4,c:1,q:"Which of the following ratios uses sales in the denominator?",o:["Days in inventory","Receivables turnover","Cash ratio","Average collection period"],a:3,x:"Average collection period = receivables ÷ (sales/365): sales is in the denominator. Receivables turnover = sales ÷ receivables (sales on top). Days in inventory uses COGS."},
{s:"F2",n:5,c:1,q:"For a levered firm, EBIT is equivalent to:",o:["Net income","Pro forma earnings","Operating profit","Net income before taxes"],a:2,x:"EBIT (earnings before interest and taxes) is operating profit."},
{s:"F2",n:6,c:1,q:"Common-size financial statements are constructed in order to:",o:["Adjust for inflation and risk","Facilitate comparisons of different-sized companies","Comply with SEC requirements","All of the above"],a:1,x:"Expressing lines as % of sales or total assets removes the effect of size."},
{s:"F2",n:7,c:1,q:"A firm has $100 of average inventory, operating profit of $500 and sales of $1,500. What is its days in inventory?",o:["36.5 days","24.3 days","73.0 days","Not enough information"],a:3,x:"Days in inventory = inventory ÷ (COGS/365). COGS is not given, so it cannot be computed. 24.3 days would wrongly use sales."},
{s:"F2",n:8,c:1,q:"For which business would you expect high asset turnover and low profit margins?",o:["Supermarkets","Banks","Software developers","Airlines"],a:0,x:"Supermarkets sell huge volumes with thin margins."},
{s:"F2",n:9,c:1,miss:true,q:"Toys by Tom, Inc. has a current ratio of ____, suggesting ____. (Simplified balance sheet and income statement given.)",o:["9.6; reasonable ability to cover interest expense","0.57; potential illiquidity","0.21; potential collection problems","1.75; reasonable liquidity"],a:1,x:"Current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities. 0.57 means current liabilities are bigger than current assets: a warning of possible illiquidity. 9.6 sounds like times interest earned, a different ratio."},
{s:"F2",n:10,c:1,miss:true,q:"What is Toys by Tom, Inc.’s return on assets (ROA)?",o:["6.9%","0.86","18%","1.2"],a:0,x:"ROA = net income ÷ total assets, which gives 6.9%. 0.86 and 1.2 are not percentages, they look like turnovers."},
{s:"F2",n:11,c:2,q:"Operating cash flow is generated by a company’s daily operations related to production and sales of goods and/or services.",o:TF,a:0,x:"True, as opposed to investing or financing cash flows."},
{s:"F2",n:12,c:2,q:"In general, the reduction of an asset is a source of funds.",o:TF,a:0,x:"True. Selling inventory or collecting receivables frees cash."},
{s:"F2",n:13,c:2,q:"The sustainable growth rate is the maximum growth rate achievable over an extended period of time.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. It is the growth possible with no new equity and constant ratios. Firms can grow faster by changing ratios or issuing shares."},
{s:"F2",n:14,c:2,q:"How is the cash conversion cycle calculated?",o:["Days in Inventory + Collection Period","Days in Inventory − Payables Period","Days in Inventory + Collection Period − Payables Period","None of the above"],a:2,x:"CCC = days in inventory + collection period − payables period."},
{s:"F2",n:15,c:2,q:"A company can shorten its cash cycle by:",o:["Reducing inventory turnover","Reducing account payables","Reducing days receivable","None of the above"],a:2,x:"Collecting faster shortens the cycle. Lower inventory turnover means more days in inventory (longer); lower payables means paying suppliers sooner (longer)."},
{s:"F2",n:16,c:2,q:"A company has a retention rate of 50%, sales of $25,000, beginning equity of $50,000, a profit margin of 10%, asset turnover of 0.75 and debt of $10,000. What is its sustainable growth rate?",o:["2.5%","1.7%","3.75%","Not enough information given"],a:0,x:"Net income = 10% × 25,000 = 2,500. ROBE = 2,500 ÷ 50,000 = 5%. SGR = 50% × 5% = 2.5%. Asset turnover and debt are distractors."},
{s:"F2",n:17,c:3,q:"Scenario analysis is a way of testing forecasts by changing one assumption at a time.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. One assumption at a time is sensitivity analysis. Scenario analysis changes several assumptions together."},
{s:"F2",n:18,c:3,q:"Biases can and should always be eliminated in financial forecasts.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. Unintended biases should be removed, but deliberate ones (a conservative or worst case) are legitimate if explained."},
{s:"F2",n:19,c:3,q:"Which of the following is commonly used in preparing pro forma statements?",o:["Historical financial statements","Projected sales","Efficiency ratios","All of the above"],a:3,x:"Pro formas start from past statements, use projected sales, and use ratios (percent of sales, efficiency ratios) to project each account."},
{s:"F2",n:20,c:3,q:"What are pro forma statements?",o:["Summaries of historical financial statements","Government-mandated analyses of financial statements","Projected statements used in financial planning","Estimated tax liabilities"],a:2,x:"Pro formas are projected financial statements used for planning."},
{s:"F2",n:21,c:4,q:"Which liabilities form part of a company’s “real” activities?<span class='stmt'>I. Short-term debt<br>II. Accounts payable<br>III. Accrued operating expenses<br>IV. Long-term debt</span>",o:["III only","II and III","I and IV","I only"],a:1,x:"Payables and accrued operating expenses come from running the business: real. Short- and long-term debt are financing: financial."},
{s:"F2",n:22,c:5,q:"The cost of debt is generally lower than the cost of equity.",o:TF,a:0,x:"True. Debt is a fixed, senior claim, so it is less risky for investors and therefore cheaper."},
{s:"F2",n:23,c:5,q:"M&amp;M’s Proposition I states that a company’s value is independent of its capital structure.",o:TF,a:0,x:"True, in perfect markets: value depends only on the assets."},
{s:"F2",n:24,c:5,q:"A higher level of leverage generally reduces managerial discretion.",o:TF,a:0,x:"True. Debt commits future cash to interest and principal and brings covenants, so managers have less free cash to spend as they wish."},
{s:"F2",n:25,c:5,q:"The Pecking Order Theory of capital structure implies a unique optimum capital structure.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. It ranks funding sources each time money is needed but gives no single optimal ratio. The static tradeoff model is the one with a unique optimum."},
{s:"F2",n:26,c:5,q:"As EBIT drops, the ROE of a levered firm drops ____ the ROE of an otherwise identical unlevered firm.",o:["the same as","relatively more than","relatively less than","more or less than (it cannot be determined)"],a:1,x:"Interest is a fixed charge, so a fall in EBIT hits a levered firm's net income harder. Leverage amplifies ROE in both directions."},
{s:"F2",n:27,c:5,q:"The owner of Grandma’s Applesauce plans to retire after the coming year. She must repay a $50,000 loan plus 8% interest from operating cash flow, which will be $65,000 (70% probability) or $45,000 (30%). Tax rate 0%. What is the owner’s expected cash flow after debt service?",o:["$9,000","$5,000","$11,000","$7,700"],a:1,x:"Debt service = 50,000 × 1.08 = 54,000. Expected cash flow = 0.7 × 65,000 + 0.3 × 45,000 = 59,000. Expected cash after debt service = 59,000 − 54,000 = $5,000.",note:"The $7,700 option assumes she pays nothing in the bad year (0.7 × 11,000). The course key is $5,000: it treats the full repayment as owed."},
{s:"F2",n:28,c:5,q:"Shareholders prefer high risk projects when facing a high probability of bankruptcy because",o:["high risk projects usually bring high rewards.","shareholders have the residual claim on a company.","creditors have the residual claim on a company, and therefore bear the risk.","there is a good chance the government will rescue them in bankruptcy."],a:1,x:"Shareholders own the residual claim with limited liability. Near bankruptcy they have little to lose, while most of the downside of a risky bet falls on creditors."},
{s:"F2",n:29,c:5,q:"The ____ states that the value of the firm is determined solely by the value of its assets.",o:["Static Tradeoff Model","M&amp;M proposition I","The Pecking Order Model","Agency Theory"],a:1,x:"M&amp;M Proposition I: value comes from the assets, not from how they are financed."},
{s:"F2",n:30,c:5,q:"Which expresses the value of a levered firm (VL) in the Static Tradeoff model? (VU = unlevered firm value, CFD = expected costs of financial distress.)",o:["VL = PV(Tax Shield) − PV(CFD)","VL = VU + PV(Tax Shield) / PV(CFD)","VL = VU + PV(Tax Shield) − PV(CFD)","VL = VU + PV(Tax Shield)"],a:2,x:"VL = VU + PV(tax shields) − PV(costs of financial distress). The last option is M&amp;M with taxes only."},
{s:"F2",n:31,c:5,q:"Which is an example of an indirect cost of bankruptcy?",o:["Court costs","Attorney and advisor fees","Lost sales due to customers and suppliers lost trust","All of the above"],a:2,x:"Indirect costs are lost business: customers, suppliers and employees who walk away. Court costs and fees are direct costs."},
{s:"F2",n:32,c:5,q:"Which of the following are equivalent under M&amp;M proposition I?",o:["Maximizing firm value and maximizing firm profit","Maximizing firm value and minimizing the cost of capital","Minimizing the cost of capital and minimizing the debt burden","Maximizing profit and minimizing taxes"],a:1,x:"Maximizing firm value and minimizing the cost of capital lead to the same capital structure."},
{s:"F2",n:33,c:5,q:"Which is NOT an assumption underlying M&amp;M proposition I?",o:["No arbitrage","No taxes","Corporate investments are risk-free","Symmetric information"],a:2,x:"M&amp;M never assumes investments are risk-free. It relies on no taxes, symmetric information and no arbitrage in equilibrium."},
{s:"F2",n:34,c:5,q:"Which trait is commonly found in debt contracts?",o:["Seniority","Covenants","Callability","All of the above"],a:3,x:"Seniority, covenants and callability are all common debt features, along with security (collateral)."},
{s:"F2",n:35,c:6,q:"Selecting investment projects according to rules based either on project NPV or IRR results in maximizing firm value.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. The NPV rule maximizes value, but IRR can mislead: several IRRs when cash flow signs change more than once, the reinvestment assumption, projects of different sizes."},
{s:"F2",n:36,c:6,q:"A dollar today is worth more than a dollar tomorrow.",o:TF,a:0,x:"True: today's dollar can be invested and earn interest."},
{s:"F2",n:37,c:6,q:"The NPV rule (invest in projects with NPV &gt; 0) depends on the assumption of value maximization.",o:TF,a:0,x:"True. Investing in every positive-NPV project is what a value maximizer does."},
{s:"F2",n:38,c:6,q:"You invest $2,000 today for three years at 5% interest paid annually and re-invest all interest. How much interest do you earn in total?",o:["205.00","300.00","315.25","500.00"],a:2,x:"FV = 2,000 × 1.05<sup>3</sup> = 2,315.25, so interest = $315.25. $300 is simple interest."},
{s:"F2",n:39,c:6,q:"The amount by which a project increases the value of the firm is given by the project’s ____.",o:["accounting rate of return","net present value (NPV)","internal rate of return (IRR)","present value"],a:1,x:"NPV = value created. Present value alone ignores the investment cost."},
{s:"F2",n:40,c:6,q:"Which items are necessary to calculate the NPV of a project?<span class='stmt'>I. Investment outlays<br>II. Discount rate<br>III. Incremental cash flow<br>IV. Time period for the project</span>",o:["I, II and IV","I, II and III","II, III and IV","All of the above"],a:3,x:"You need all four: what you invest, the rate, the incremental cash flows and when each occurs."},
{s:"F2",n:41,c:6,q:"Compute the NPV of an investment with 5 years of annual inflows of $100 and two outflows: $100 today and $50 at the beginning of the second year. Discount rate 10%.",o:["$229.08","$287.60","$233.62","$271.53"],a:2,x:"NPV = −100 − 50 ÷ 1.10 + 100 × (1/1.1 + 1/1.1<sup>2</sup> + … + 1/1.1<sup>5</sup>) = −100 − 45.45 + 379.08 = $233.62. The beginning of year 2 is the end of year 1, so the $50 is discounted once."},
{s:"F2",n:42,c:6,q:"A riskless project requires $10 now and pays $30 once, two years later. The risk-free rate is 5%. Which statement is NOT true?",o:["The net present value of the project is positive.","The IRR is greater than 50 percent.","The accounting rate of return on the project is positive.","The payback period is less than 2 years."],a:3,x:"NPV = 30 ÷ 1.05<sup>2</sup> − 10 = 17.2 &gt; 0. IRR: 10 × (1 + r)<sup>2</sup> = 30, r = 73% &gt; 50%. Accounting return is positive. Nothing comes back before year 2, so payback is exactly 2 years, not less."},
{s:"F2",n:43,c:6,q:"What is the present value of a perpetuity of $100 given a discount rate of 5%?",o:["$2,000","$3,000","$1,500","$500"],a:0,x:"PV = CF ÷ r = 100 ÷ 0.05 = $2,000."},
{s:"F2",n:44,c:7,q:"All else equal, when a company’s debt ratio rises, its beta falls.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. More debt makes equity riskier, so the equity beta rises (β equity = β unlevered ÷ E/V)."},
{s:"F2",n:45,c:7,q:"If you borrow capital to start a business and the money is provided interest-free, then your cost of capital is zero.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. The cost of capital is an opportunity cost: what you could earn elsewhere at the same risk."},
{s:"F2",n:46,c:7,q:"Increasing a company’s leverage has no effect on its cost of equity.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. More leverage makes equity riskier and raises its cost (Golden State 14.0% vs Smucker 9.7%, same unlevered beta)."},
{s:"F2",n:47,c:7,q:"Which assumptions about investor behavior are required by the CAPM?<span class='stmt'>I. Investors try to maximize their wealth<br>II. Investors consider only risk when making investments<br>III. Investors are risk averse<br>IV. Investors adopt a long-term perspective</span>",o:["I and III","I, II and III","I and IV","All of the above"],a:0,x:"I and III. Investors consider risk and return (not only risk), and a long-term perspective is not an assumption."},
{s:"F2",n:48,c:7,q:"For a firm with an optimal capital structure, the WACC is:",o:["higher than the cost of equity","lower than the cost of debt","lower than the cost of unlevered equity","independent of the capital structure"],a:2,x:"With debt, the interest tax shield pulls the WACC below the cost of unlevered equity (Golden State: WACC 8.67% &lt; R assets 8.95%)."},
{s:"F2",n:49,c:7,q:"Which information is NOT required to calculate the WACC of a company with debt?",o:["Its capital structure ratios","Its cost of debt","Its current ratio","Its tax rate"],a:2,x:"WACC needs D/V, E/V, Kd, Ke and the tax rate. The current ratio plays no part."},
{s:"F2",n:50,c:7,q:"In the CAPM, what does beta measure?",o:["Non-systematic (diversifiable) risk","Systematic (non-diversifiable) risk","Total risk","Risk-adjusted stock returns"],a:1,x:"Beta measures systematic risk: how much the stock moves with the market."},

/* ===== Pre-test ===== */
{s:"PT",n:1,c:1,q:"Asset turnover is equal to assets divided by sales.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. Asset turnover = sales ÷ assets."},
{s:"PT",n:2,c:1,q:"The equity to asset ratio is equal to [1 – the debt to asset ratio].",o:TF,a:0,x:"True. Assets = liabilities + equity, so equity/assets = 1 − liabilities/assets."},
{s:"PT",n:3,c:1,miss:true,q:"Firm XYZ has the following sales data. What is the compound average growth rate (CAGR) for the full period?",o:["5.43%","–7.17%","–5.43%","7.17%"],a:1,x:"CAGR = (last ÷ first)<sup>1/n</sup> − 1, with n the number of years between them. A negative result means sales shrank on average."},
{s:"PT",n:4,c:1,q:"Which of the following common ratios measures efficiency?",o:["Return on assets","Times interest earned","Days in inventory","Contribution margin"],a:2,x:"Days in inventory is an efficiency ratio. ROA is profitability, times interest earned is leverage."},
{s:"PT",n:5,c:2,q:"All else equal, a firm with higher days in inventory will have a shorter cash cycle.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. More days in inventory make the cash cycle longer."},
{s:"PT",n:6,c:1,q:"Firm ABC has assets of $80, sales of $75 and COGS of $40. Current assets of $50 consist of cash, receivables and inventory. Current liabilities are $60. Cash is $20 and receivables $10. In a common size balance sheet, inventory equals:",o:["37.5%","25.0%","40.0%","26.7%"],a:1,x:"Inventory = 50 − 20 − 10 = 20. Common size = 20 ÷ total assets 80 = 25.0%."},
{s:"PT",n:7,c:1,q:"Firm BCD has an average collection period of 93 days. If accounts receivable are $242, what are its sales (nearest dollar)?",o:["$62","$140","$950","$1433"],a:2,x:"Collection period = receivables ÷ (sales/365), so sales = 242 × 365 ÷ 93 = $950."},
{s:"PT",n:8,c:1,q:"Holding all else constant, which changes increase the interval measure?<span class='stmt'>I. Cash increases<br>II. Receivables increase<br>III. Operating costs increase</span>",o:["I","I and II","I and III","I, II and III"],a:1,x:"Interval measure = (cash + securities + receivables) ÷ daily operating costs. More cash or receivables raise it; higher operating costs lower it."},
{s:"PT",n:9,c:1,q:"Which statements about the compound average growth rate are true?<span class='stmt'>I. CAGR is the single constant growth rate that, compounded over a finite number of years, produces the observed end point from the observed starting value.<br>II. CAGR generally measures the arithmetic average of sales growth over a multi-year period.<br>III. Over a multi-year period, CAGR can be positive even if most of the one-year growth rates are negative.</span>",o:["I only","I and II","I and III","I, II, and III"],a:2,x:"I is the definition and III is true because only the endpoints matter. II is false: CAGR is a geometric (compounded) average, not arithmetic."},
{s:"PT",n:10,c:1,q:"A firm has receivables turnover of 8. Sales are $600. What is the value of average receivables?",o:["$75","$480","$750","$4800"],a:0,x:"Receivables turnover = sales ÷ receivables, so receivables = 600 ÷ 8 = $75."},
{s:"PT",n:11,c:2,q:"The cash cycle may also be described as:<span class='stmt'>I. The operating cycle<br>II. The net working capital cycle<br>III. A process that is largely unaffected by managerial choices</span>",o:["I only","II only","I and II only","I, II, and III"],a:3,x:"I and II are other names for the cash cycle. The course key also counts III as true.",note:"Careful: the tutorial text says managerial choices (credit terms, inventory policy) do affect the cycle. The official key for this question is still “I, II, and III”."},
{s:"PT",n:12,c:2,q:"Which of the following is an example of a use of funds?",o:["Increase in inventory","Increase in long-term debt","Reduction in cash","Sale of fixed assets"],a:0,x:"Increasing an asset absorbs cash: use. More debt, less cash and selling fixed assets are sources."},
{s:"PT",n:13,c:2,q:"A firm has days in inventory of 110 and a receivables collection period of 64 days. Its cash conversion cycle is 27 days. What is its payables period?",o:["19 days","73 days","147 days","201 days"],a:2,x:"CCC = DIO + collection − payables. 27 = 110 + 64 − payables, so payables period = 147 days."},
{s:"PT",n:14,c:2,fig:"BIS,BBS",q:"Presented below are Billie’s Baubles’ 2019 statements. Assume credit purchases equal COGS. Billie’s Baubles collects its receivables faster than it pays its payables, which worsens its cash conversion cycle.",o:TF,a:1,x:"Collection period = 70 ÷ (400/365) = 64 days. Payables period = 40 ÷ (100/365) = 146 days. It does collect faster than it pays, but that shortens the cycle: it improves it. False."},
{s:"PT",n:15,c:2,q:"In 2007 Joe’s Shoes had receivables of $40 and payables of $30. In 2008, receivables of $25 and payables of $45. The decrease in receivables is a ____ of cash and the increase in payables is a ____ of cash.",o:["use; use","use; source","source; source","source; use"],a:2,x:"An asset decrease is a source, and a liability increase is a source too."},
{s:"PT",n:16,c:2,fig:"BIS,BBS",q:"Presented below are Billie’s Baubles’ 2019 statements. For 2020, Billie wants to increase sales growth. Which projected 2020 balance sheet data are most consistent with this goal (all other accounts about the same)?",o:["Inventory: $25; accounts receivable: $75; bank debt: $50","Inventory: $30; accounts receivable: $70; bank debt: $40","Inventory: $35; accounts receivable: $65; bank debt: $35","Inventory: $40; accounts receivable: $75; bank debt: $50"],a:3,x:"Growth needs more inventory and more receivables, financed with more bank debt. Only the last option raises all three (30 → 40, 70 → 75, 40 → 50)."},
{s:"PT",n:17,c:3,q:"Yannie’s Yoga Clothing has 60 mall stores; its competitor Tina’s Threads has stores in the same malls. If Tina’s closes half its stores, the likely impact on Yannie’s statements (scenario analysis, total market unchanged) is:",o:["Decrease in sales; decrease in cost of goods sold","Increase in sales; increase in cost of goods sold","Increase in sales; no change in cost of goods sold","No change to sales; no change to cost of goods sold"],a:1,x:"Tina's customers come to Yannie: sales rise, and COGS rises with them because COGS varies with sales."},
{s:"PT",n:18,c:3,q:"Some balance sheet and income statement accounts that vary directly with sales include:<span class='stmt'>I. Cost of goods sold<br>II. Depreciation<br>III. Accounts payable<br>IV. Accounts receivable</span>",o:["I, II, III only","I, II, IV only","I, III, IV only","I, II, III, and IV"],a:2,x:"COGS, payables and receivables move with sales. Depreciation depends on fixed assets."},
{s:"PT",n:19,c:3,q:"Which statement about a pro forma balance sheet is true?",o:["It does not need to follow the usual accounting rules","It is a projection of a company’s assets, liabilities, and equity","Its main focus is on the method by which a firm might raise additional funding","It is a projection of a company’s revenues, costs, and profit"],a:1,x:"A pro forma balance sheet projects assets, liabilities and equity, following normal accounting rules. Revenues and costs are in the pro forma income statement."},
{s:"PT",n:20,c:4,q:"Which is a real activity that generates cash by producing goods and services for customers?",o:["Debt issuance","Equity issuance","Research and development expense","Interest expense"],a:2,x:"R&amp;D is part of producing goods and services. Issuing debt or shares and paying interest are financial."},
{s:"PT",n:21,c:4,fig:"BBS",q:"Presented below is Billie’s Baubles’ 2019 balance sheet. Which should be reclassified as real activities?<span class='stmt'>I. Accounts payable<br>II. Other accrued liabilities<br>III. Revolving bank debt</span>",o:["I only","II only","I and II only","I, II, and III"],a:2,x:"Payables and accrued liabilities come from operations: real. Revolving bank debt is interest-bearing financing: financial."},
{s:"PT",n:22,c:5,q:"Which statement about common equity is true?",o:["Common equity generally has a lower cost of capital than debt.","Common equity holders vote on day-to-day firm decisions.","Common equity represents a shareholder’s residual claim on the firm’s cash flow.","Common equity holders have priority over preferred equity holders to receive dividends."],a:2,x:"Equity is the residual claim. It costs more than debt, shareholders vote on the board (not daily decisions), and preferred shareholders are paid first."},
{s:"PT",n:23,c:5,q:"Which is NOT a condition of M&amp;M’s Proposition I?",o:["No costs of financial distress","No taxes","Incomplete markets: investors and companies have different borrowing and lending opportunities","Symmetric information"],a:2,x:"M&amp;M requires complete markets (same borrowing and lending opportunities for all). Incomplete markets is the opposite."},
{s:"PT",n:24,c:5,q:"The “Conservation of Business Risk” implies that a change in capital structure changes the business risk per dollar of equity, but not the total amount of risk.",o:TF,a:0,x:"True. Debt does not change the risk of the operations; it concentrates it on fewer equity dollars."},
{s:"PT",n:25,c:5,q:"An investor bought Billie’s Baubles stock last year at $47. Today the price is $53. If Billie’s pays a $2 dividend per share right now, today’s price will be ____, dividend income ____, and unrealized capital gain ____.",o:["$45; $2; $4","$51; $0; $6","$51; $2; $4","$53; $2; $4"],a:2,x:"The price drops by the dividend: 53 − 2 = 51. Dividend income = $2. Unrealized gain = 51 − 47 = $4."},
{s:"PT",n:26,c:5,q:"In a world with no taxes, shareholders are indifferent between dividend payouts and share repurchases.",o:TF,a:0,x:"True. Without taxes both leave shareholders with the same total wealth."},
{s:"PT",n:27,c:5,fig:"BIS",q:"Billie’s Baubles had net worth of $150 in 2018. What was its return on beginning equity in 2019?",o:["54.2%","68.7%","98.1%","171.7%"],a:1,x:"ROBE = 2019 net income ÷ 2018 net worth = 103 ÷ 150 = 68.7%."},
{s:"PT",n:28,c:5,q:"Which is an example of a direct cost of financial distress?",o:["Distracted management","Loss of customers","Loss of employees","Attorney fees"],a:3,x:"Attorney fees are direct. The other three are indirect costs."},
{s:"PT",n:29,c:5,q:"The static tradeoff model relaxes two conditions of M&amp;M Proposition I:<span class='stmt'>I. Taxes<br>II. Costs of financial distress<br>III. Symmetric information<br>IV. Complete markets</span>",o:["I and III","I and II","II and III","III and IV"],a:1,x:"Taxes and costs of financial distress."},
{s:"PT",n:30,c:5,fig:"BIS",q:"Billie’s Baubles’ tax rate is 35%. What was the tax shield from interest expense in 2019?",o:["$2.45","$4.55","$7.00","$14.00"],a:0,x:"Interest tax shield = interest × tax rate = 7 × 35% = $2.45."},
{s:"PT",n:31,c:5,q:"Ideally, managers select a capital structure that ____ the value of the firm and/or ____ the firm’s cost of capital.",o:["maximizes; maximizes","maximizes; minimizes","minimizes; maximizes","minimizes; minimizes"],a:1,x:"Maximize value, minimize the cost of capital: the same goal seen two ways."},
{s:"PT",n:32,c:5,q:"The ____ relaxes the M&amp;M assumption of symmetric information by assuming insiders know more than outsiders, leading outsiders to charge more for capital.",o:["Agency cost theory","Pecking order hypothesis","Product market model","Static tradeoff model"],a:1,x:"The pecking order hypothesis is built on asymmetric information."},
{s:"PT",n:33,c:5,q:"Which statements are true?<span class='stmt'>I. It is legally permissible for a firm with cash flow problems to skip a planned interest payment.<br>II. It is legally permissible for a firm with cash flow problems to skip a planned dividend payment.</span>",o:["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],a:1,x:"Skipping interest is a default. Skipping a dividend is allowed: the board decides dividends."},
{s:"PT",n:34,c:5,q:"The ____ argues that firm managers have interests that differ from their investors, so they may not work hard enough, pay less attention, or steal from investors.",o:["Agency cost theory","Pecking order hypothesis","Product market model","Static tradeoff model"],a:0,x:"Agency cost theory: agents (managers) do not always act for principals (owners)."},
{s:"PT",n:35,c:6,q:"Using a discount rate of 8%, Billie estimates a new project’s NPV at –$40. The project’s IRR ____ the discount rate of 8%.",o:["must be lower than","must be higher than","must be equal to","cannot be definitively compared to"],a:0,x:"NPV falls as the rate rises. NPV is already negative at 8%, so the rate that makes it zero is below 8%."},
{s:"PT",n:36,c:6,q:"All else equal, companies prefer shorter payback periods to longer payback periods.",o:TF,a:0,x:"True: money back sooner is worth more and is less risky."},
{s:"PT",n:37,c:6,q:"If an investment has a ____ NPV, its IRR is typically ____ the discount rate.",o:["negative; greater than","positive; greater than","positive; less than","positive; equal to"],a:1,x:"Positive NPV usually means IRR above the discount rate."},
{s:"PT",n:38,c:6,q:"Which investment decision criterion does not require estimating the cost of capital?",o:["Net present value","Payback period","Discounted payback period","Internal rate of return"],a:1,x:"Simple payback adds undiscounted cash flows, so it needs no rate. NPV, discounted payback and IRR (compared with the cost of capital) all need one."},
{s:"PT",n:39,c:6,q:"A concern with NPV is that under certain circumstances it is possible to have more than one NPV for a single project.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. The concern of multiple values applies to IRR (when cash flow signs change more than once). NPV has one value for a given rate."},
{s:"PT",n:40,c:8,fig:"BIS",q:"Billie’s tax rate was 35% in 2019. Capital expenditures were $30 and the change in net working capital was $25. What was Billie’s Free Cash Flow for 2019?",o:["$31.05","$61.95","$87.95","$92.50"],a:3,x:"FCF = EBIT(1 − t) + depreciation − capex − ΔNWC = 150 × 0.65 + 50 − 30 − 25 = 97.5 + 50 − 55 = $92.50."},
{s:"PT",n:41,c:6,q:"With a 36% tax rate, what is the annual depreciation tax shield of a $100,000 machine depreciated straight-line over 8 years?",o:["$2,880","$3,600","$4,500","$6,400"],a:2,x:"Depreciation = 100,000 ÷ 8 = 12,500 a year. Tax shield = 12,500 × 36% = $4,500."},
{s:"PT",n:42,c:6,q:"You want $25,000 in 5 years and earn 8% compounded annually. How much must you invest today?",o:["$5,000.00","$17,014.58","$18,375.75","$23,148.15"],a:1,x:"PV = 25,000 ÷ 1.08<sup>5</sup> = 25,000 ÷ 1.4693 = $17,014.58."},
{s:"PT",n:43,c:6,q:"An investment pays $500 per year forever at a 5% rate. What is its present value?",o:["$100","$2,500","$10,000","$1,000,000"],a:2,x:"Perpetuity PV = CF ÷ r = 500 ÷ 0.05 = $10,000."},
{s:"PT",n:44,c:7,miss:true,q:"Billie’s manager has estimated possible cash flows and their probabilities for a new project. What is the expected (probability-weighted) cash flow?",o:["$32.50","$35.00","$38.00","$40.00"],a:2,x:"Expected cash flow = Σ (probability × cash flow): multiply each outcome by its probability and add them up. Result: $38."},
{s:"PT",n:45,c:7,q:"In the capital allocation decision, what is the definition of the cost of capital?",o:["0%","the return you can receive on an investment with similar risk","the current risk-free rate","the return on a risky bond"],a:1,x:"Cost of capital = opportunity cost: the return available elsewhere at similar risk."},
{s:"PT",n:46,c:7,q:"The standard deviation of a portfolio of two risky assets is the weighted average of each asset’s standard deviation.",o:TF,a:1,x:"False. The expected return is a weighted average, but the standard deviation is lower whenever correlation is below 1. That is diversification."},
{s:"PT",n:47,c:7,q:"The portfolio that offers the highest level of return for a given level of risk is called the ____.",o:["Efficient frontier","Minimum variance portfolio","Optimal portfolio","Maximum variance portfolio"],a:2,x:"The course's answer is the optimal portfolio. The efficient frontier is the whole curve of such portfolios, not one portfolio."},
{s:"PT",n:48,c:7,fig:"CML",q:"On the tangent line in the plot, investors in portfolios to the left of the market portfolio are ____ of riskless Treasury securities, while investors to the right are ____.",o:["borrowers; borrowers","borrowers; lenders","lenders; borrowers","lenders; lenders"],a:2,x:"Left of the market portfolio, investors hold some Treasuries: they lend. Right of it, they borrow at the risk-free rate to invest more than 100% in the market."},
{s:"PT",n:49,c:7,q:"The risk-free rate is 5%, stock A has a beta of 1.2, and the expected return on the market is 12%. What is the expected return of stock A?",o:["8.4%","13.4%","17.0%","19.4%"],a:1,x:"E(r) = 5% + 1.2 × (12% − 5%) = 5% + 8.4% = 13.4%. The market premium is 7%, not 12%."},
{s:"PT",n:50,c:7,q:"Using a short-term Treasury bill’s yield as the risk-free rate, what is the beta of the risk-free asset?",o:["0","0.25","0.50","1.00"],a:0,x:"A T-bill does not move with the market: β = 0."}
];

/* ---------- state ---------- */
var KEY="cf-midterm-answers-v1";
var ans={};
try{ ans=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY)||"{}")||{}; }catch(e){ ans={}; }
function save(){ try{ localStorage.setItem(KEY,JSON.stringify(ans)); }catch(e){} }
var st={src:"all",ch:"all",view:"all"};
function id(q){ return q.s+"-"+q.n; }
var FIG={BIS:BIS,BBS:BBS,XYZ:XYZ,CML:CML};
var ORDER={PT:0,F1:1,F2:2};
Q.sort(function(a,b){ return a.c-b.c || ORDER[a.s]-ORDER[b.s] || a.n-b.n; });

/* chapter counts in Learn */
var counts={}; Q.forEach(function(q){ counts[q.c]=(counts[q.c]||0)+1; });
document.querySelectorAll("[data-count]").forEach(function(el){ var c=el.getAttribute("data-count"); el.textContent=(counts[c]||0)+" exam questions on this chapter"; });

/* ---------- filters ---------- */
function chip(group,val,label){
  var b=document.createElement("button"); b.type="button"; b.className="chip"; b.textContent=label;
  b.setAttribute("data-g",group); b.setAttribute("data-v",val);
  b.addEventListener("click",function(){ st[group]=val; render(); });
  return b;
}
var fsrc=document.getElementById("fsrc"), fch=document.getElementById("fch"), fview=document.getElementById("fview");
[["all","All 150"],["PT","Pre-test"],["F1","Final Exam 1"],["F2","Final Exam 2"]].forEach(function(x){ fsrc.appendChild(chip("src",x[0],x[1])); });
fch.appendChild(chip("ch","all","All"));
for(var c=1;c<=8;c++){ fch.appendChild(chip("ch",String(c),c+" · "+CH[c])); }
[["all","Everything"],["todo","Not answered yet"],["missed","My mistakes"]].forEach(function(x){ fview.appendChild(chip("view",x[0],x[1])); });

function letter(i){ return "ABCD".charAt(i); }

function cardHTML(q){
  var k=id(q), my=ans[k], done=(my!==undefined);
  var h='<div class="meta"><span class="src">'+SRC[q.s]+' · Q'+q.n+'</span><span>Ch. '+q.c+' · '+CH[q.c]+'</span></div>';
  h+='<div class="q">'+q.q+'</div>';
  if(q.fig){ q.fig.split(",").forEach(function(f){ h+=FIG[f]; }); }
  if(q.miss){ h+='<p class="note">'+MISSING+'</p>'; }
  h+='<div class="opts">';
  q.o.forEach(function(t,i){
    var cls="opt";
    if(done){ if(i===q.a) cls+=" right"; else if(i===my) cls+=" wrong"; }
    h+='<button type="button" class="'+cls+'" data-q="'+k+'" data-i="'+i+'"'+(done?' disabled':'')+'><span class="k">'+letter(i)+'</span><span>'+t+'</span></button>';
  });
  h+='</div>';
  if(done){
    if(my===-1){ h+='<div class="verdict">Answer: '+letter(q.a)+'</div>'; }
    else if(my===q.a){ h+='<div class="verdict ok">Correct</div>'; }
    else { h+='<div class="verdict ko">Not quite. The answer is '+letter(q.a)+'.</div>'; }
    h+='<div class="why">'+q.x+'</div>';
    if(q.note){ h+='<p class="note">'+q.note+'</p>'; }
    h+='<div class="cardfoot"><button type="button" class="linkbtn" data-retry="'+k+'">Try again</button></div>';
  } else {
    h+='<div class="cardfoot"><button type="button" class="linkbtn" data-reveal="'+k+'">Show the answer</button></div>';
  }
  return h;
}

function filtered(){
  return Q.filter(function(q){
    if(st.src!=="all" && q.s!==st.src) return false;
    if(st.ch!=="all" && String(q.c)!==st.ch) return false;
    var my=ans[id(q)];
    if(st.view==="todo" && my!==undefined) return false;
    if(st.view==="missed" && !(my!==undefined && my!==q.a)) return false;
    return true;
  });
}

function render(){
  document.querySelectorAll(".chip").forEach(function(b){ b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(st[b.getAttribute("data-g")]===b.getAttribute("data-v"))); });
  var list=filtered(), box=document.getElementById("cards");
  box.innerHTML="";
  if(!list.length){ box.innerHTML='<div class="empty">'+(st.view==="missed"?"No mistakes in this selection.":st.view==="todo"?"You have answered every question in this selection.":"No questions here.")+'</div>'; }
  list.forEach(function(q){ var d=document.createElement("article"); d.className="card"; d.id="q-"+id(q); d.innerHTML=cardHTML(q); box.appendChild(d); });
  score();
}

function score(){
  var scope=Q.filter(function(q){ return (st.src==="all"||q.s===st.src)&&(st.ch==="all"||String(q.c)===st.ch); });
  var answered=0, right=0;
  scope.forEach(function(q){ var m=ans[id(q)]; if(m!==undefined && m!==-1){ answered++; if(m===q.a) right++; } });
  var pct=answered?Math.round(100*right/answered):0;
  document.getElementById("score").innerHTML='<span><b>'+answered+'</b> / '+scope.length+' answered</span><span><b>'+right+'</b> correct</span><span><b>'+pct+'%</b> score</span>';
  document.getElementById("bar").style.width=(scope.length?Math.round(100*answered/scope.length):0)+"%";
}

function redrawCard(k){
  var q=Q.filter(function(x){ return id(x)===k; })[0], el=document.getElementById("q-"+k);
  if(q && el){ el.innerHTML=cardHTML(q); }
  score();
}

document.getElementById("cards").addEventListener("click",function(e){
  var o=e.target.closest(".opt");
  if(o && !o.disabled){ var k=o.getAttribute("data-q"); ans[k]=Number(o.getAttribute("data-i")); save(); redrawCard(k); return; }
  var r=e.target.closest("[data-reveal]");
  if(r){ var k2=r.getAttribute("data-reveal"); ans[k2]=-1; save(); redrawCard(k2); return; }
  var t=e.target.closest("[data-retry]");
  if(t){ var k3=t.getAttribute("data-retry"); delete ans[k3]; save(); redrawCard(k3); }
});

var resetBtn=document.getElementById("reset"), armed=false, timer=null;
resetBtn.addEventListener("click",function(){
  if(!armed){ armed=true; resetBtn.textContent="Click again to erase all answers"; timer=setTimeout(function(){ armed=false; resetBtn.textContent="Reset my answers"; },4000); return; }
  clearTimeout(timer); armed=false; ans={}; save(); resetBtn.textContent="Answers erased"; setTimeout(function(){ resetBtn.textContent="Reset my answers"; },1800); render();
});

/* ---------- modes ---------- */
var MODES=["learn","practice","formulas"];
function setMode(m){
  MODES.forEach(function(x){ document.getElementById(x).hidden=(x!==m); });
  document.querySelectorAll(".modes a").forEach(function(a){ if(a.getAttribute("data-mode")===m) a.setAttribute("aria-current","page"); else a.removeAttribute("aria-current"); });
  document.getElementById("index").hidden=(m!=="learn");
  document.getElementById("wrap").classList.toggle("solo", m!=="learn");
  if(m==="practice") render();
}
function route(){
  var h=(location.hash||"").replace("#","");
  if(MODES.indexOf(h)>=0){ setMode(h); window.scrollTo(0,0); return; }
  if(/^ch[1-8]$/.test(h) || h==="start"){ setMode("learn"); var el=document.getElementById(h); if(el) el.scrollIntoView(); return; }
  setMode("learn");
}
window.addEventListener("hashchange",route);
document.querySelectorAll("[data-goch]").forEach(function(b){
  b.addEventListener("click",function(){ st.ch=b.getAttribute("data-goch"); st.src="all"; st.view="all"; if(location.hash==="#practice"){ setMode("practice"); window.scrollTo(0,0); } else { location.hash="practice"; } });
});
route();
})();
</script>

</body></html>
